window.COURSE = {"readme": "# HashiCorp Terraform Associate Certification — Course & Training Repo\n\nA self-paced course to prepare for the **HashiCorp Certified: Terraform Associate (004)** exam.\nEvery lesson pairs a written lesson with a **hands-on lab that runs locally, with no cloud account and no cost**\n(labs use the `local`, `random`, `null`, `time` and built-in `terraform_data` providers/resources).\n\n> Always check the [official exam page](https://developer.hashicorp.com/certifications/infrastructure-automation)\n> before your exam: objectives, product version and pricing can change.\n\n## Exam at a glance\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Exam code | Terraform Associate (004) |\n| Format | Online, proctored, multiple choice / multiple select / true-false |\n| Duration | 1 hour |\n| Terraform version tested | Terraform 1.12 (004 exam) |\n| Validity | 2 years |\n| Prerequisites | Basic terminal skills, basic understanding of on-prem and cloud architecture |\n\n## Repository layout\n\n```\n.\n├── README.md                  ← you are here\n├── STUDY-PLAN.md              ← 4-week schedule (and a 1-week crash plan)\n├── EXAM-OBJECTIVES.md         ← every objective mapped to a lesson\n├── lessons/\n│   ├── 01-iac-concepts/\n│   ├── 02-terraform-fundamentals/\n│   ├── 03-core-workflow/\n│   ├── 04-terraform-configuration/\n│   ├── 05-modules/\n│   ├── 06-state-management/\n│   ├── 07-maintain-infrastructure/\n│   └── 08-hcp-terraform/\n│       ├── README.md          ← the lesson + review quiz\n│       └── lab/               ← runnable Terraform code + LAB.md instructions\n├── practice-exams/            ← two full-length practice exams with explained answers\n├── cheatsheets/               ← CLI, HCL, and state/backends quick references\n├── flashcards.md              ← rapid-recall Q&A\n├── site/                      ← interactive training website (see below)\n└── scripts/\n    ├── validate-labs.sh       ← runs fmt/init/validate on every lab\n    ├── build-site.py          ← bundles the Markdown into site/content.js\n    └── lab-server.py          ← serves the site + in-browser lab terminals\n```\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. Install Terraform ≥ 1.12: <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install>\n   ```bash\n   terraform version\n   ```\n2. Clone/open this repo and start with [lesson 01](lessons/01-iac-concepts/README.md).\n3. For each lesson:\n   - Read `README.md`\n   - Do the lab in `lab/` (instructions in `lab/LAB.md`)\n   - Answer the review quiz at the bottom of the lesson\n4. After lesson 08, take [practice exam 1](practice-exams/practice-exam-1.md), review weak areas,\n   then take [practice exam 2](practice-exams/practice-exam-2.md). Aim for **≥ 85%** before booking.\n\n## Lessons\n\n| # | Lesson | Exam objective |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01 | [Infrastructure as Code concepts](lessons/01-iac-concepts/README.md) | 1 |\n| 02 | [Terraform fundamentals: providers, install, versions](lessons/02-terraform-fundamentals/README.md) | 2 |\n| 03 | [The core Terraform workflow](lessons/03-core-workflow/README.md) | 3 |\n| 04 | [Terraform configuration (HCL deep dive)](lessons/04-terraform-configuration/README.md) | 4 |\n| 05 | [Modules](lessons/05-modules/README.md) | 5 |\n| 06 | [State management](lessons/06-state-management/README.md) | 6 |\n| 07 | [Maintaining infrastructure](lessons/07-maintain-infrastructure/README.md) | 7 |\n| 08 | [HCP Terraform](lessons/08-hcp-terraform/README.md) | 8 |\n\n## Training website\n\nThe whole course is also available as an interactive website in [`site/`](site/): lessons and labs,\ntimed practice exams (or study mode with instant explanations), flashcards with mastery tracking,\na clickable objectives checklist, full-text search, and a progress dashboard. Progress is saved in your browser.\n\n### Do the labs in your browser\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/lab-server.py          # then open http://localhost:8000\n```\n\nThe lab server serves the site and turns each lesson's **Hands-on lab** tab into a workbench:\nthe instructions on one side, and on the other real terminals (bash + Terraform on your machine)\nand a file editor. Click ▶ next to any command in the instructions to run it. Each lab runs in a\nsandbox copy under `.lab-workspace/<lesson>/` (gitignored), so the course files stay untouched.\nUse **Reset lab** to start a lab over.\n\n- Requires Linux, macOS or WSL, plus Terraform on your `PATH`.\n- Listens on `127.0.0.1` only, and API calls need a per-run token, so other websites can't reach your shell.\n- `python3 scripts/lab-server.py --reset` deletes all lab sandboxes and clears the saved progress in your\n  browser the next time you open the site. The dashboard's **Reset all progress** button does the same.\n\n### Static mode\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/build-site.py          # regenerate site/content.js after editing any Markdown\npython3 -m http.server -d site 8000    # then open http://localhost:8000\n```\n\nOpening `site/index.html` directly also works. Without the lab server, labs show their instructions for\nyou to follow in your own terminal. The static site can be hosted on GitHub Pages by publishing the `site/` folder.\n\n## Validate all labs\n\n```bash\n./scripts/validate-labs.sh\n```\n\n## Official resources\n\n- Exam content list & review guide: <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-associate-tutorials-004>\n- Terraform docs: <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs>\n- Terraform language: <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language>\n- HCP Terraform: <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cloud-docs>\n- Terraform Registry: <https://registry.terraform.io>\n\n## Disclaimer\n\nThis is an independent study resource, not affiliated with or endorsed by HashiCorp.\nPractice questions are original and written to exercise the objectives; they are not real exam questions.\n", "studyPlan": "# Study Plan\n\n## 4-week plan (≈ 1 hour/day, weekdays)\n\n| Week | Day | Work |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Mon | Install Terraform. Read [Lesson 01](lessons/01-iac-concepts/README.md), do its lab |\n| 1 | Tue | [Lesson 02](lessons/02-terraform-fundamentals/README.md) reading |\n| 1 | Wed | Lesson 02 lab (providers, lock file, multiple provider configs) |\n| 1 | Thu | [Lesson 03](lessons/03-core-workflow/README.md) reading + lab |\n| 1 | Fri | Review quizzes 01–03, [CLI cheatsheet](cheatsheets/cli.md), flashcards 1–30 |\n| 2 | Mon | [Lesson 04](lessons/04-terraform-configuration/README.md) part 1: resources, data, variables, outputs |\n| 2 | Tue | Lesson 04 part 2: types, expressions, functions, `for`, `dynamic` |\n| 2 | Wed | Lesson 04 part 3: meta-arguments, dependencies, custom conditions, sensitive data |\n| 2 | Thu | Lesson 04 lab (all parts) |\n| 2 | Fri | Lesson 04 quiz + [HCL cheatsheet](cheatsheets/hcl.md); experiment in `terraform console` |\n| 3 | Mon | [Lesson 05](lessons/05-modules/README.md) reading |\n| 3 | Tue | Lesson 05 lab |\n| 3 | Wed | [Lesson 06](lessons/06-state-management/README.md) reading |\n| 3 | Thu | Lesson 06 lab (state commands, `moved`, `removed`, backends) |\n| 3 | Fri | [Lesson 07](lessons/07-maintain-infrastructure/README.md) reading + lab (import, logging) |\n| 4 | Mon | [Lesson 08](lessons/08-hcp-terraform/README.md) reading; optionally sign up for HCP Terraform free tier and do the lab |\n| 4 | Tue | [Practice exam 1](practice-exams/practice-exam-1.md) — timed (60 min) |\n| 4 | Wed | Review every wrong answer, re-read the lessons concerned |\n| 4 | Thu | [Practice exam 2](practice-exams/practice-exam-2.md) — timed |\n| 4 | Fri | [Flashcards](flashcards.md) full pass; book the exam if scoring ≥ 85% |\n\n## 1-week crash plan (experienced users)\n\n| Day | Work |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Skim lessons 01–03, do labs 02 and 03 |\n| 2 | Lesson 04 in full + lab |\n| 3 | Lessons 05 + 06 + labs |\n| 4 | Lessons 07 + 08 |\n| 5 | Practice exam 1, review |\n| 6 | Practice exam 2, review, flashcards |\n| 7 | Rest; light flashcard review; take the exam |\n\n## Exam-day tips\n\n- Read every word: \"**NOT**\", \"**best**\", \"**first**\", \"select **two**\".\n- Mark hard questions and come back; there is no penalty for guessing, so never leave a blank.\n- Most questions test *what Terraform does by default* — know the defaults (local backend, `terraform.tfstate`,\n  `-parallelism=10`, lock file committed, `.terraform/` not committed, etc.).\n- Command-behavior questions are common: know exactly what `init`, `validate`, `plan`, `apply`,\n  `fmt`, `state`, `import`, `output`, `show`, `console`, `workspace`, `login` do and **don't** do.\n", "objectives": "# Exam Objectives → Lesson Map (Terraform Associate 004)\n\nTick each box once you can explain the topic *and* have done it in a lab.\n\n## 1. Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform → [Lesson 01](lessons/01-iac-concepts/README.md)\n- [ ] 1a Explain what IaC is\n- [ ] 1b Describe the advantages of IaC patterns\n- [ ] 1c Explain how Terraform manages multi-cloud, hybrid cloud, and service-agnostic workflows\n\n## 2. Terraform fundamentals → [Lesson 02](lessons/02-terraform-fundamentals/README.md)\n- [ ] 2a Install and version Terraform providers\n- [ ] 2b Describe how Terraform uses providers\n- [ ] 2c Write Terraform configuration using multiple providers\n- [ ] 2d Explain how Terraform uses and manages state\n\n## 3. Core Terraform workflow → [Lesson 03](lessons/03-core-workflow/README.md)\n- [ ] 3a Describe the Terraform workflow (Write → Plan → Apply)\n- [ ] 3b Initialize a working directory (`terraform init`)\n- [ ] 3c Validate a configuration (`terraform validate`)\n- [ ] 3d Generate and review an execution plan (`terraform plan`)\n- [ ] 3e Apply changes (`terraform apply`)\n- [ ] 3f Destroy infrastructure (`terraform destroy`)\n- [ ] 3g Apply formatting and style adjustments (`terraform fmt`)\n\n## 4. Terraform configuration → [Lesson 04](lessons/04-terraform-configuration/README.md)\n- [ ] 4a Use and differentiate resource and data blocks\n- [ ] 4b Refer to resource attributes and create cross-resource references\n- [ ] 4c Use variables and outputs\n- [ ] 4d Understand and use complex types\n- [ ] 4e Write dynamic configuration using expressions and functions\n- [ ] 4f Define resource dependencies\n- [ ] 4g Validate configuration using custom conditions\n- [ ] 4h Understand best practices for managing sensitive data, including secrets management with Vault\n\n## 5. Terraform modules → [Lesson 05](lessons/05-modules/README.md)\n- [ ] 5a Explain how Terraform sources modules\n- [ ] 5b Describe variable scope within modules\n- [ ] 5c Use modules in configuration\n- [ ] 5d Manage module versions\n\n## 6. Terraform state management → [Lesson 06](lessons/06-state-management/README.md)\n- [ ] 6a Describe the local backend\n- [ ] 6b Describe state locking\n- [ ] 6c Configure remote state using the `backend` block\n- [ ] 6d Manage resource drift and Terraform state\n\n## 7. Maintain infrastructure with Terraform → [Lesson 07](lessons/07-maintain-infrastructure/README.md)\n- [ ] 7a Import existing infrastructure into your Terraform workspace\n- [ ] 7b Use the CLI to inspect state\n- [ ] 7c Describe when and how to use verbose logging\n\n## 8. HCP Terraform → [Lesson 08](lessons/08-hcp-terraform/README.md)\n- [ ] 8a Use HCP Terraform to create infrastructure\n- [ ] 8b Describe HCP Terraform collaboration and governance features\n- [ ] 8c Describe how to organize and use HCP Terraform workspaces and projects\n- [ ] 8d Configure and use HCP Terraform integration\n\n> Source: the official 004 exam content list. Wording is paraphrased; verify against\n> <https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-associate-tutorials-004>.\n", "lessons": [{"id": "01-iac-concepts", "num": "01", "title": "Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform", "dir": "lessons/01-iac-concepts", "md": "# Lesson 01 — Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform\n\n**Objectives covered:** 1a, 1b, 1c · **Time:** ~45 min\n\n## 1. What is Infrastructure as Code?\n\nInfrastructure as Code is managing and provisioning infrastructure (networks, VMs, DNS records, databases,\nSaaS settings…) through **machine-readable definition files** instead of manual processes such as clicking\nthrough a web console or running one-off scripts.\n\nTwo main styles:\n\n| Style | You describe… | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Declarative** | the *desired end state*; the tool figures out the steps | Terraform, CloudFormation, Kubernetes manifests |\n| **Imperative / procedural** | the *exact steps* to run in order | Bash scripts, most SDK scripts |\n\n**Terraform is declarative.** You write *what* you want; Terraform compares that to reality (via state)\nand computes *how* to get there (the plan).\n\nRelated distinction:\n\n- **Provisioning tools** (Terraform) create the infrastructure itself.\n- **Configuration management tools** (Ansible, Chef, Puppet) configure software *on* existing machines.\n  They can overlap, but Terraform's sweet spot is provisioning.\n\n- **Mutable** infrastructure is changed in place (patching a server).\n- **Immutable** infrastructure is replaced rather than changed (build a new image, swap servers).\n  Terraform encourages immutable patterns: many attribute changes force a **replace** (destroy + create).\n\n## 2. Advantages of IaC\n\n| Benefit | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| **Version control** | Infra lives in Git: history, diffs, code review, rollback |\n| **Repeatability / consistency** | The same code creates identical dev/stage/prod environments; eliminates config drift from manual changes |\n| **Automation & speed** | Environments created in minutes; fits into CI/CD |\n| **Self-documenting** | The code *is* the documentation of what exists |\n| **Collaboration** | Teams review changes before they happen (plan output in PRs) |\n| **Reusability** | Modules package best practices for reuse |\n| **Reduced risk** | Preview changes (`terraform plan`) before applying; fewer human errors |\n| **Cost & lifecycle control** | Easy to destroy temporary environments |\n\n## 3. How Terraform works (the big picture)\n\n```\n ┌────────────┐   terraform plan/apply    ┌──────────────┐   API calls   ┌──────────────────┐\n │ .tf files  │ ────────────────────────▶ │ Terraform    │ ────────────▶ │ AWS / Azure /    │\n │ (desired)  │                           │ Core + state │               │ GCP / GitHub /   │\n └────────────┘                           └──────┬───────┘               │ Kubernetes / ... │\n                                                 │ gRPC                   └──────────────────┘\n                                          ┌──────▼───────┐\n                                          │  Providers   │ (plugins)\n                                          └──────────────┘\n```\n\n- **Terraform Core** — the `terraform` binary. Reads config, manages state, builds the dependency graph,\n  computes the plan, and calls providers.\n- **Providers** — plugins that translate Terraform resources into API calls for a specific platform.\n- **State** — Terraform's record mapping config resources to real-world objects (Lesson 06).\n\nTerraform is written in Go, ships as a single binary, and configuration is written in **HCL**\n(HashiCorp Configuration Language) or, less commonly, JSON (`*.tf.json`).\n\n## 4. Multi-cloud, hybrid cloud, and service-agnostic workflows\n\n- **Multi-cloud:** one tool, one language, one workflow to manage AWS, Azure, GCP, etc. in the same\n  configuration. Resources from different providers can reference each other (e.g. create a VM in AWS and a DNS\n  record in Cloudflare pointing to it).\n- **Hybrid cloud:** providers exist for on-prem platforms (vSphere, Nutanix, OpenStack) as well as public clouds.\n- **Service-agnostic:** providers exist for thousands of non-infrastructure APIs: GitHub, Datadog, PagerDuty,\n  Kubernetes, Helm, Okta, Vault… If it has an API, there's probably a provider.\n\nImportant nuance for the exam: Terraform is **cloud-agnostic as a tool**, but configurations are **not portable**\nbetween clouds. An `aws_instance` cannot be \"switched\" to Azure; you write `azurerm_linux_virtual_machine` instead.\nWhat you keep is the *workflow*, language, and tooling.\n\n## 5. Terraform editions (know the names)\n\n| Edition | What |\n|---|---|\n| **Terraform Community Edition (CLI)** | Free binary; you manage state and execution yourself |\n| **HCP Terraform** (formerly *Terraform Cloud*) | SaaS: remote state, remote runs, VCS integration, private registry, policies, team access |\n| **Terraform Enterprise** | Self-hosted distribution of HCP Terraform |\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Terraform = **declarative**, **provisioning**, **agentless**, **cloud-agnostic workflow**.\n- Plan before apply → predictable changes.\n- State lets Terraform map config to real resources and compute diffs.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. Is Terraform declarative or imperative? What does that mean?\n2. Name four benefits of IaC.\n3. True/False: A Terraform configuration written for AWS can be applied to Azure by changing the provider.\n4. What component translates Terraform resources into platform API calls?\n5. Which is the self-hosted version of HCP Terraform?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. Declarative — you describe the desired end state and Terraform works out the steps.\n2. Any four of: version control, repeatability, automation, self-documentation, collaboration, reuse, reduced risk via plan, consistency across environments.\n3. **False** — resource types are provider-specific. The *workflow* is portable, the code is not.\n4. A **provider** (plugin).\n5. **Terraform Enterprise**.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 01 — Your first declarative configuration\n\n**Goal:** feel the difference between declarative and imperative, and see idempotency.\n\n## Steps\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/01-iac-concepts/lab\nterraform init\nterraform apply        # type \"yes\"\ncat output/hello.txt\n```\n\n1. Run `terraform apply` **again**. Notice: `No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.`\n   That's **idempotency** — declaring the same desired state twice does nothing the second time.\n2. Change `var.greeting` default in `main.tf` to something else and run `terraform plan`.\n   Notice `-/+ must be replaced` — the `local_file` resource can't be modified in place, so Terraform\n   replaces it (immutable pattern).\n3. Delete `output/hello.txt` manually, then `terraform plan`. Terraform detects the file is gone\n   (drift) and plans to recreate it.\n4. Compare with `imperative.sh`: run it twice. It blindly repeats its steps; it has no concept of\n   current vs. desired state.\n5. Clean up:\n   ```bash\n   terraform destroy\n   ```\n\n## What you learned\n\n- Declarative config + state = idempotent, drift-aware changes.\n- Some changes force replacement.\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "imperative.sh", "content": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Imperative equivalent: a list of steps with no knowledge of current state.\n# Run it twice and notice it just repeats the work (and appends a duplicate line).\nset -euo pipefail\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\nmkdir -p output\necho \"Creating file...\"\necho \"Hello, imperative world!\" >> output/imperative.txt\necho \"Done. Contents:\"\ncat output/imperative.txt\n"}, {"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"greeting\" {\n  description = \"Text written to the file\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"Hello, Infrastructure as Code!\"\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"hello\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/hello.txt\"\n  content  = \"${var.greeting}\\n\"\n}\n\noutput \"file_path\" {\n  value = local_file.hello.filename\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "02-terraform-fundamentals", "num": "02", "title": "Terraform Fundamentals: Providers & State Basics", "dir": "lessons/02-terraform-fundamentals", "md": "# Lesson 02 — Terraform Fundamentals: Providers & State Basics\n\n**Objectives covered:** 2a, 2b, 2c, 2d · **Time:** ~1.5 h\n\n## 1. Installing Terraform\n\nTerraform is a single Go binary. Install options: package managers (`brew`, `apt`/`yum` via the HashiCorp repo,\n`choco`), manual zip download, or version managers such as `tfenv`/`tfswitch`.\n\n```bash\nterraform version        # shows core version + provider versions in the current dir\nterraform -help          # list commands\nterraform -install-autocomplete\n```\n\n## 2. The `terraform` settings block\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0, < 2.0.0\"   # constrains the Terraform CLI version\n\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"           # <HOSTNAME>/<NAMESPACE>/<TYPE>\n      version = \"~> 6.0\"                    # constrains the provider version\n    }\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \">= 3.6.0\"\n    }\n  }\n\n  backend \"s3\" { ... }     # optional: where state is stored (Lesson 06)\n  # cloud { ... }          # optional: HCP Terraform integration (Lesson 08)\n}\n```\n\n- Only **constants** are allowed inside the `terraform` block (no variables, no functions).\n- **Source address:** `[<HOSTNAME>/]<NAMESPACE>/<TYPE>`. Hostname defaults to `registry.terraform.io`.\n  So `hashicorp/aws` == `registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws`.\n- If you omit `required_providers`, Terraform infers `hashicorp/<name>` from the resource prefix —\n  works for official providers only; always declare explicitly.\n\n### Version constraint operators (very testable!)\n\n| Operator | Meaning | Example `~> 1.2.0` / `~> 1.2` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `=` (or none) | exactly this version | `= 1.2.0` |\n| `!=` | exclude a version | `!= 1.2.3` |\n| `>`, `>=`, `<`, `<=` | comparisons | `>= 1.2.0, < 2.0.0` |\n| `~>` | **pessimistic**: only the *rightmost* component may increase | `~> 1.2.0` → `>= 1.2.0, < 1.3.0`<br>`~> 1.2` → `>= 1.2, < 2.0` |\n\n## 3. Providers — how Terraform uses them\n\n- Providers are **plugins**, separate binaries, released on their own schedule, independent of Terraform core.\n- Terraform Core talks to providers over **RPC (gRPC)**.\n- Each provider defines **resource types** and **data sources** (e.g. `aws_instance`, `data.aws_ami`).\n- `terraform init` **downloads** required providers into `.terraform/providers/` in the working directory.\n- Provider tiers on the Registry:\n  - **Official** — owned & maintained by HashiCorp (`hashicorp/` namespace)\n  - **Partner** — maintained by a technology partner, verified by HashiCorp\n  - **Community** — published by individuals/organizations\n  - (Archived — no longer maintained)\n\n### Provider configuration\n\n```hcl\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"eu-west-1\"\n  # credentials: use env vars / shared config / IAM roles — never hard-code secrets\n}\n```\n\nA provider block is optional if the provider needs no configuration (e.g. `random`, `local`).\n\n### Multiple configurations of the same provider — `alias`\n\n```hcl\nprovider \"aws\" {             # default configuration (no alias)\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n}\n\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  alias  = \"west\"\n  region = \"us-west-2\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" {\n  provider = aws.west        # <PROVIDER>.<ALIAS>\n  bucket   = \"my-logs-west\"\n}\n```\n\nResources without a `provider` argument use the **default** (un-aliased) configuration.\nPassing aliased providers into modules uses the `providers = { aws = aws.west }` argument (Lesson 05).\n\n### Multiple *different* providers in one configuration\n\nTotally normal — e.g. `aws` + `cloudflare` + `random` in the same root module, with\ncross-provider references (`cloudflare_record.value = aws_instance.web.public_ip`).\n\n## 4. The dependency lock file: `.terraform.lock.hcl`\n\n- Created/updated by `terraform init`.\n- Records the **exact provider versions** selected and their **checksums (hashes)**.\n- **Commit it to version control** so everyone (and CI) uses the same provider versions.\n- Only tracks **providers**, **not modules**.\n- On subsequent `init`, Terraform reuses the locked versions even if newer ones satisfy constraints.\n- To move to newer allowed versions: `terraform init -upgrade`.\n- To add hashes for other OSes: `terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64`.\n\n## 5. Plugin cache & provider installation\n\n- Default: providers downloaded per working directory into `.terraform/`.\n- Optional shared cache: `plugin_cache_dir` in the CLI config file (`~/.terraformrc`) or env var\n  `TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR`.\n- Air-gapped: `provider_installation` block with `filesystem_mirror` / `network_mirror`, or\n  `terraform providers mirror <dir>`.\n\n## 6. How Terraform uses state (intro — deep dive in Lesson 06)\n\nState (`terraform.tfstate`, JSON) is Terraform's **source of truth about what it manages**:\n\n1. **Mapping** config resources → real-world object IDs.\n2. **Metadata**, e.g. dependencies, so Terraform can destroy in the right order even after resources were removed from config.\n3. **Performance** — caches attribute values; for big infra you can use `-refresh=false`.\n4. **Syncing** — shared remote state lets teams collaborate.\n\nFacts to memorize:\n- Default: **local backend**, file `terraform.tfstate` in the working directory; previous version kept in `terraform.tfstate.backup`.\n- State can contain **secrets in plain text** → protect it, never commit it to Git.\n- Terraform **refreshes** state (reads real infrastructure) during `plan` and `apply` by default.\n- Don't edit state by hand; use `terraform state` subcommands.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `terraform { required_providers {...} }` + `.terraform.lock.hcl` = reproducible provider versions.\n- `~> 1.2.0` allows patch updates only; `~> 1.2` allows minor updates.\n- `alias` = multiple configurations of the same provider.\n- State maps config to real resources; stored locally by default.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. What does `~> 5.4.0` allow? And `~> 5.4`?\n2. Which file records exact provider versions and should be committed to Git?\n3. Which command upgrades providers to the newest versions allowed by constraints?\n4. How do you make a resource use a non-default provider configuration?\n5. Does the lock file track module versions?\n6. What is the default source hostname for providers?\n7. Name the three main provider tiers.\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `~> 5.4.0` → `>= 5.4.0, < 5.5.0`. `~> 5.4` → `>= 5.4, < 6.0`.\n2. `.terraform.lock.hcl`.\n3. `terraform init -upgrade`.\n4. Declare the provider with `alias = \"x\"`, then set `provider = <name>.x` on the resource.\n5. No — providers only.\n6. `registry.terraform.io`.\n7. Official, Partner, Community.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 02 — Providers, versions, the lock file, and aliases\n\n## Part A — Providers & the lock file\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/02-terraform-fundamentals/lab\nterraform init\nls -la .terraform/providers/registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/\ncat .terraform.lock.hcl\nterraform version\nterraform providers          # shows the provider requirement tree\n```\n\nQuestions:\n1. Which exact versions of `random` and `local` were selected? Where do you see the hashes?\n2. Change the `random` constraint in `main.tf` to `\"~> 3.5.0\"` and run `terraform init`.\n   What error do you get, and why? (The lock file pins a version outside the new constraint.)\n3. Now run `terraform init -upgrade`. What changed in the lock file?\n4. Restore the original constraint and run `terraform init -upgrade` again.\n\n## Part B — Multiple provider configurations with `alias`\n\nOpen `aliases.tf`. It declares a default and an aliased configuration of the `random` provider.\n`random` takes no arguments, so this is **syntax practice**. The mechanics are the same as the classic\nmulti-region `aws` example shown in the comments.\n\n```bash\nterraform apply\nterraform output\n```\n\nLook at `terraform state list` — note `random_pet.primary` and `random_pet.secondary`, the latter created\nthrough the aliased provider configuration `random.secondary`.\n\n## Part C — Inspect state\n\n```bash\ncat terraform.tfstate | head -40\nterraform show\n```\n\nFind: `\"version\"`, `\"terraform_version\"`, `\"serial\"`, `\"lineage\"`, and each resource's `\"provider\"` string.\nThen run `terraform apply` with a change and check that `serial` increments and `terraform.tfstate.backup` appears.\n\n## Clean up\n\n```bash\nterraform destroy\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "aliases.tf", "content": "# Syntax practice for provider aliases.\n# In real life this is typically:\n#   provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east-1\" }\n#   provider \"aws\" { alias = \"west\"  region = \"us-west-2\" }\n# The random provider has no arguments, but the alias mechanics are identical.\n\nprovider \"random\" {\n  # default (un-aliased) configuration\n}\n\nprovider \"random\" {\n  alias = \"secondary\"\n}\n\nresource \"random_pet\" \"secondary\" {\n  provider = random.secondary # <PROVIDER NAME>.<ALIAS>\n  length   = 3\n}\n\noutput \"secondary_name\" {\n  value = random_pet.secondary.id\n}\n"}, {"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# Two different providers used together, with a cross-provider reference:\n# the random provider generates a name, the local provider writes it to disk.\nresource \"random_pet\" \"primary\" {\n  length    = 2\n  separator = \"-\"\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"server_name\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/server-name.txt\"\n  content  = \"server: ${random_pet.primary.id}\\n\"\n}\n\noutput \"primary_name\" {\n  value = random_pet.primary.id\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "03-core-workflow", "num": "03", "title": "The Core Terraform Workflow", "dir": "lessons/03-core-workflow", "md": "# Lesson 03 — The Core Terraform Workflow\n\n**Objectives covered:** 3a–3g · **Time:** ~1 h\n\n## 1. Write → Plan → Apply\n\n| Stage | Individual | Team | HCP Terraform |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Write** | Edit `.tf` in your editor | Branch + edit | Branch + edit |\n| **Plan** | `terraform plan` locally, iterate | Plan output posted in a pull request for review | Speculative plan runs on the PR automatically |\n| **Apply** | `terraform apply` | Merge, then apply from CI | Merge triggers a run; apply after approval |\n\nThe full CLI lifecycle: `init` → (`fmt`, `validate`) → `plan` → `apply` → … → `destroy`.\n\n## 2. `terraform init`\n\nInitializes a working directory. **Always the first command** and safe to run many times (idempotent).\n\nIt:\n- Initializes the **backend** (where state lives).\n- Downloads **providers** → `.terraform/providers/` and writes/updates `.terraform.lock.hcl`.\n- Downloads **modules** → `.terraform/modules/`.\n- Does **not** create any infrastructure and does **not** create a state file for the local backend.\n\nMust be re-run when you: add/change a provider or module source/version, or change the backend.\n\nUseful flags:\n| Flag | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `-upgrade` | Upgrade providers and modules to newest allowed versions |\n| `-reconfigure` | Reconfigure backend, ignoring existing configuration (no state migration) |\n| `-migrate-state` | Migrate state to a new backend |\n| `-backend=false` | Skip backend initialization |\n| `-backend-config=path_or_kv` | Partial backend configuration |\n| `-get=false` | Skip module download |\n\n## 3. `terraform validate`\n\n- Checks **syntax** and **internal consistency** (types, required args, references to undeclared variables).\n- Requires `init` first (needs provider schemas).\n- Does **not** access remote services, state, or check whether values are valid for the real API\n  (e.g. won't catch an invalid AMI ID).\n- `terraform validate -json` for CI.\n\n## 4. `terraform fmt`\n\n- Rewrites files to the **canonical HCL style** (indentation, alignment of `=`).\n- Only formats the current directory by default; `-recursive` for subdirectories.\n- `-check` → exit code non-zero if files need formatting (doesn't modify) — perfect for CI.\n- `-diff` → show changes.\n- Does **not** validate logic.\n\n## 5. `terraform plan`\n\nCreates an **execution plan**:\n1. Reads current state.\n2. **Refreshes** — queries providers for the real status of managed objects.\n3. Compares desired config vs. current state → proposes create/update/replace/destroy.\n\nPlan symbols:\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `+` | create |\n| `-` | destroy |\n| `~` | update in-place |\n| `-/+` | destroy then create (replace) |\n| `+/-` | create then destroy (replace with `create_before_destroy`) |\n| `<=` | read (data source) |\n\nUseful flags:\n| Flag | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `-out=tfplan` | Save the plan to a file for a later exact `apply` |\n| `-destroy` | Plan a destroy |\n| `-refresh-only` | Only update state to match reality; propose no infra changes |\n| `-refresh=false` | Skip refresh (faster, riskier) |\n| `-target=ADDR` | Focus on a resource (exceptional use only) |\n| `-replace=ADDR` | Force replacement of a resource |\n| `-var 'k=v'` / `-var-file=f.tfvars` | Set variables |\n| `-detailed-exitcode` | Exit 0 = no changes, 1 = error, 2 = changes present |\n| `-parallelism=n` | Concurrent operations (default **10**) |\n| `-generate-config-out=file.tf` | Generate config for `import` blocks (Lesson 07) |\n\n`plan` **never modifies real infrastructure**. (A saved plan file can contain sensitive values — protect it.)\n\n## 6. `terraform apply`\n\n- Without a plan file: creates a new plan, shows it, and asks for **`yes`** confirmation.\n- `-auto-approve` skips the prompt (CI only).\n- `terraform apply tfplan` applies a **saved plan** without prompting and without re-planning.\n  If state changed since the plan was made, Terraform rejects it as stale.\n- Accepts most plan flags (`-var`, `-target`, `-replace`, `-refresh-only`, `-parallelism`).\n- If apply fails partway, resources already created remain and are recorded in state;\n  Terraform does **not** roll back. Fix and apply again.\n- Resources whose creation-time provisioner fails are marked **tainted** and replaced next apply.\n\n## 7. `terraform destroy`\n\n- Destroys **all** resources tracked in the current state/workspace.\n- Alias for `terraform apply -destroy`. Preview with `terraform plan -destroy`.\n- Asks for confirmation (`-auto-approve` to skip).\n- To destroy just one resource: remove it from the config and apply (preferred) or `-target` (exceptional).\n\n## 8. Other workflow commands worth knowing\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `terraform show` | Human-readable view of state or a saved plan (`-json` for machines) |\n| `terraform output [name]` | Print outputs from state (`-raw`, `-json`) |\n| `terraform console` | Interactive REPL to evaluate expressions and functions |\n| `terraform graph` | Emits dependency graph in DOT format |\n| `terraform providers` | Show provider requirements |\n| `terraform get` | Download/update modules only |\n| `terraform test` | Run `.tftest.hcl` tests |\n| `terraform login` / `logout` | Obtain/remove an API token for HCP Terraform |\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `init` first; re-run after provider/module/backend changes.\n- `validate` = offline syntax + consistency; `fmt` = style only.\n- `plan` refreshes by default and never changes infra; `apply` can use a saved plan (no prompt).\n- `destroy` = `apply -destroy`.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. Which command downloads providers and modules?\n2. Does `terraform validate` check whether an AMI ID exists?\n3. Which `fmt` flag is used in CI to fail the pipeline on unformatted code?\n4. What does `-/+` mean in plan output?\n5. You ran `terraform plan -out=tfplan`. What happens with `terraform apply tfplan`?\n6. What is the default value of `-parallelism`?\n7. An apply fails after creating 3 of 5 resources. What happens to the 3?\n8. What's the equivalent of `terraform destroy` using `apply`?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `terraform init` (also `terraform get` for modules only).\n2. No — validate never contacts remote APIs.\n3. `terraform fmt -check` (often with `-recursive`).\n4. The resource will be destroyed then re-created (replacement).\n5. It applies exactly that plan, **without** a confirmation prompt.\n6. 10.\n7. They remain and are recorded in state; there is no automatic rollback.\n8. `terraform apply -destroy`.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 03 — init, fmt, validate, plan, apply, destroy\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/03-core-workflow/lab\n```\n\n## 1. fmt\n`main.tf` is intentionally badly formatted.\n```bash\nterraform fmt -check ; echo \"exit code: $?\"   # non-zero: needs formatting\nterraform fmt -diff\nterraform fmt -check ; echo \"exit code: $?\"   # 0 now\n```\n\n## 2. validate before init\n```bash\nterraform validate      # fails: providers not installed yet\nterraform init\nterraform validate      # Success!\n```\nNow break something: change `length = var.pet_length` to `length = var.does_not_exist` and validate again.\nUndo the change.\n\n## 3. plan\n```bash\nterraform plan\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform show tfplan\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode ; echo \"exit code: $?\"   # 2 = changes pending\n```\n\n## 4. apply\n```bash\nterraform apply tfplan          # no prompt — saved plan\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode ; echo \"exit code: $?\"   # 0 = no changes\nterraform apply -var 'pet_length=4'   # interactive; notice the replacement (-/+)\nterraform output\nterraform output -raw pet_name\n```\n\n## 5. replace & target\n```bash\nterraform apply -replace='random_pet.this'     # force replacement of one resource\nterraform plan -target='local_file.pet'        # note the warning about -target\n```\n\n## 6. console & graph\n```bash\nterraform console\n> random_pet.this.id\n> upper(random_pet.this.id)\n> length(split(\"-\", random_pet.this.id))\n> exit\nterraform graph   # paste into https://dreampuf.github.io/GraphvizOnline to visualize\n```\n\n## 7. destroy\n```bash\nterraform plan -destroy\nterraform destroy\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "main.tf", "content": "# This file is INTENTIONALLY badly formatted. Run `terraform fmt` to fix it.\nterraform {\nrequired_providers {\nrandom = {\nsource = \"hashicorp/random\"\nversion = \"~> 3.6\"\n}\nlocal = {\n    source = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n}\n}\n}\n\nvariable \"pet_length\" {\ntype = number\n    default = 2\n}\n\nresource \"random_pet\" \"this\" {\nlength = var.pet_length\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"pet\" {\n  filename    = \"${path.module}/output/pet.txt\"\n    content = \"My pet is ${random_pet.this.id}\\n\"\n}\n\noutput \"pet_name\" {\nvalue = random_pet.this.id\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "04-terraform-configuration", "num": "04", "title": "Terraform Configuration (HCL Deep Dive)", "dir": "lessons/04-terraform-configuration", "md": "# Lesson 04 — Terraform Configuration (HCL Deep Dive)\n\n**Objectives covered:** 4a–4h · **Time:** ~4 h (the largest exam domain — take it in 3 sittings)\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — Blocks, resources, data sources, references\n\n### 1.1 HCL syntax\n\n```hcl\n<BLOCK TYPE> \"<LABEL>\" \"<LABEL>\" {\n  <IDENTIFIER> = <EXPRESSION>   # argument\n  nested_block { ... }\n}\n```\n\n- Comments: `#` or `//` single line, `/* */` multi-line. `#` is the idiomatic one.\n- Files: `*.tf` (HCL) or `*.tf.json`. All `.tf` files in a directory form **one module** — file names don't matter\n  (convention: `main.tf`, `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf`, `providers.tf`, `versions.tf`, `terraform.tf`).\n- `*_override.tf` / `override.tf` files merge into and override other definitions (rarely used).\n\n### 1.2 Resource blocks — things Terraform **manages** (create/update/delete)\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {          # type \"aws_instance\", local name \"web\"\n  ami           = \"ami-123456\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n}\n```\n- Address: `aws_instance.web`. Type + name must be unique within a module.\n- Type prefix (`aws_`) tells Terraform which provider to use.\n\n### 1.3 Data sources — things Terraform **reads** (never manages)\n\n```hcl\ndata \"aws_ami\" \"ubuntu\" {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [\"099720109477\"]\n  filter {\n    name   = \"name\"\n    values = [\"ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-*-24.04-amd64-server-*\"]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  ami = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id          # data.<TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE>\n}\n```\n- Use for: looking up IDs, existing resources created elsewhere, remote state (`terraform_remote_state`),\n  computed documents (`aws_iam_policy_document`), etc.\n- Read during **plan** when possible (`<=` in plan), or deferred to apply if they depend on unknown values.\n\n### 1.4 References (cross-resource)\n\n| Syntax | Refers to |\n|---|---|\n| `<TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTR>` | managed resource attribute |\n| `data.<TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTR>` | data source attribute |\n| `var.<NAME>` | input variable |\n| `local.<NAME>` | local value |\n| `module.<NAME>.<OUTPUT>` | child module output |\n| `path.module`, `path.root`, `path.cwd` | filesystem paths |\n| `terraform.workspace` | current workspace name |\n| `count.index`, `each.key`, `each.value`, `self` | context-specific |\n\n- **Arguments** are what you set; **attributes** include those plus values *computed* by the provider\n  after creation (e.g. `id`, `arn`, `public_ip`). Unknown-until-apply values show as `(known after apply)`.\n- Referencing another resource creates an **implicit dependency**.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — Variables, outputs, locals\n\n### 2.1 Input variables\n\n```hcl\nvariable \"instance_type\" {\n  description = \"EC2 instance type\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"t3.micro\"      # no default => required\n  sensitive   = false\n  nullable    = false\n  ephemeral   = false           # 1.10+: value is not persisted to state/plan\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"t3.micro\", \"t3.small\"], var.instance_type)\n    error_message = \"Only t3.micro or t3.small are allowed.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Variable precedence — lowest to highest (LATER WINS):**\n\n1. `default` in the variable block\n2. Environment variables `TF_VAR_<name>` (e.g. `export TF_VAR_instance_type=t3.small`)\n3. `terraform.tfvars`\n4. `terraform.tfvars.json`\n5. `*.auto.tfvars` / `*.auto.tfvars.json` (lexical order of filenames)\n6. `-var` and `-var-file` on the command line (in the order given — last one wins)\n\nIf a required variable has no value from any source, Terraform **prompts** interactively\n(or errors with `-input=false`).\n\nOnly `terraform.tfvars` and `*.auto.tfvars` are loaded **automatically**; other `.tfvars` files need `-var-file`.\n\n### 2.2 Output values\n\n```hcl\noutput \"instance_ip\" {\n  description = \"Public IP\"\n  value       = aws_instance.web.public_ip\n  sensitive   = true            # redacted in CLI output, but STILL stored in state in plain text\n  depends_on  = [aws_eip.web]   # rarely needed\n}\n```\n- Outputs of the **root module** are printed after apply and via `terraform output`.\n- Outputs of a **child module** are how a module exposes values to its parent (`module.x.instance_ip`).\n- `terraform output -raw name` → raw string, handy in scripts; `-json` shows sensitive values.\n\n### 2.3 Local values\n\n```hcl\nlocals {\n  name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${var.env}\"\n  common_tags = { Project = var.project, Env = var.env }\n}\n# use: local.name_prefix   (note: block is `locals`, reference is `local.`)\n```\nUse locals to avoid repetition; they're scoped to the module and can't be set from outside.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 — Types and complex types\n\n| Kind | Types |\n|---|---|\n| Primitive | `string`, `number`, `bool` |\n| Collection (same element type) | `list(T)`, `set(T)`, `map(T)` |\n| Structural (mixed types) | `object({ attr = T, ... })`, `tuple([T1, T2, ...])` |\n| Special | `any` (let Terraform infer), `null` (absence of value) |\n\n```hcl\nvariable \"subnets\" {\n  type = list(object({\n    name = string\n    cidr = string\n    public = optional(bool, false)   # optional attribute with default\n  }))\n}\n\nvariable \"tags\" {\n  type = map(string)\n}\n```\n\n- `list` — ordered, indexed `var.l[0]`, duplicates allowed.\n- `set` — unordered, unique, **no index access** (convert with `tolist()`).\n- `map` — string keys → values of one type, `var.m[\"key\"]` or `var.m.key`.\n- `object` — fixed attribute names, each with its own type.\n- `tuple` — fixed length, each position its own type.\n- Terraform performs **automatic type conversion** where possible (`\"5\"` ↔ `5`, `\"true\"` ↔ `true`).\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 — Expressions and functions\n\n### 4.1 Expressions\n\n```hcl\n# String interpolation & directives\n\"Hello, ${var.name}!\"\n\"%{ if var.env == \"prod\" }PRODUCTION%{ else }non-prod%{ endif }\"\n\n# Heredoc\n<<-EOT\n  indented heredoc, leading whitespace trimmed\nEOT\n\n# Conditional (ternary)\ninstance_type = var.env == \"prod\" ? \"m5.large\" : \"t3.micro\"\n\n# for expressions\n[for s in var.names : upper(s)]                          # → list\n{for s in var.names : s => length(s)}                    # → map\n[for s in var.names : s if s != \"admin\"]                 # filter\n{for k, v in var.tags : k => v if v != \"\"}\n\n# Splat\naws_instance.web[*].id          # == [for i in aws_instance.web : i.id]\n\n# Operators: + - * / %  == !=  < > <= >=  && || !\n```\n\n### 4.2 `dynamic` blocks — generate repeated **nested blocks**\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" {\n  name = \"web\"\n\n  dynamic \"ingress\" {\n    for_each = var.ingress_ports          # list or map\n    content {\n      from_port   = ingress.value         # iterator is named after the block (\"ingress\")\n      to_port     = ingress.value\n      protocol    = \"tcp\"\n      cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\nUse `iterator = port` to rename the iterator variable. Don't overuse — hurts readability.\n\n### 4.3 Built-in functions (you **cannot** define your own functions in HCL; provider-defined functions exist via `provider::<name>::<fn>`)\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| String | `upper`, `lower`, `format`, `join`, `split`, `replace`, `trimspace`, `substr`, `regex` |\n| Numeric | `min`, `max`, `abs`, `ceil`, `floor` |\n| Collection | `length`, `element`, `lookup`, `merge`, `concat`, `flatten`, `keys`, `values`, `contains`, `distinct`, `toset`, `tolist`, `tomap`, `zipmap`, `slice`, `coalesce` |\n| Encoding | `jsonencode`, `jsondecode`, `yamlencode`, `base64encode` |\n| Filesystem | `file`, `fileexists`, `templatefile`, `filebase64` |\n| Date/time | `timestamp`, `formatdate`, `timeadd` |\n| Hash/crypto | `md5`, `sha256`, `uuid` |\n| IP network | `cidrsubnet`, `cidrhost` |\n| Type conversion | `tostring`, `tonumber`, `try`, `can` |\n\nTest them in `terraform console`:\n```\n> cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 2)\n\"10.0.2.0/24\"\n> lookup({a = 1}, \"b\", 0)\n0\n> element([\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"], 4)\n\"b\"                 # wraps around\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 5 — Meta-arguments & dependencies\n\nMeta-arguments work on (almost) any resource/module: `count`, `for_each`, `depends_on`, `provider`, `lifecycle`\n(and `providers` for modules).\n\n### 5.1 `count`\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  count = 3\n  tags  = { Name = \"web-${count.index}\" }\n}\n# addresses: aws_instance.web[0], [1], [2]; all: aws_instance.web[*].id\n```\nConditional creation: `count = var.create ? 1 : 0`.\nDownside: removing an element from the middle **shifts indexes**, causing unwanted replacements.\n\n### 5.2 `for_each` (map or set of strings)\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_iam_user\" \"u\" {\n  for_each = toset([\"alice\", \"bob\"])\n  name     = each.key                 # for sets, each.key == each.value\n}\n# addresses: aws_iam_user.u[\"alice\"], aws_iam_user.u[\"bob\"]\n```\nStable addresses by key → preferred when instances aren't identical. Can't use `count` and `for_each` together.\nKeys must be known at plan time.\n\n### 5.3 Dependencies\n- **Implicit** — created automatically by references. Preferred.\n- **Explicit** — `depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy.x]`, only when there's a hidden dependency Terraform\n  can't see (e.g. an app needs an IAM policy attached before it starts, but doesn't reference it).\n- Terraform builds a **dependency graph** and creates independent resources **in parallel**.\n\n### 5.4 `lifecycle`\n```hcl\nlifecycle {\n  create_before_destroy = true            # new created before old destroyed\n  prevent_destroy       = true            # plan errors if resource would be destroyed\n  ignore_changes        = [tags]          # ignore drift/changes on these attrs (or `all`)\n  replace_triggered_by  = [aws_ecs_task_definition.app.id]\n  precondition  { ... }\n  postcondition { ... }\n}\n```\nNote: `prevent_destroy` doesn't stop you deleting the block from config and applying.\n\n### 5.5 `terraform_data` (replaces `null_resource`) and provisioners\n- `terraform_data` — built-in resource for storing values / triggering replacements (`triggers_replace`).\n- **Provisioners** (`local-exec`, `remote-exec`, `file`) are a **last resort**. Prefer cloud-init/`user_data`,\n  images built with Packer, or config management tools.\n  - Creation-time by default; `when = destroy` for destroy-time.\n  - `on_failure = continue | fail` (default `fail` → resource is **tainted**).\n\n---\n\n## Part 6 — Custom conditions (validation)\n\n| Mechanism | Where | Evaluated | Effect on failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `validation` block | `variable` | when the variable is evaluated | error, blocks run |\n| `precondition` | `lifecycle` of resource / data / output | **before** the object is evaluated | error, blocks run |\n| `postcondition` | `lifecycle` of resource / data | **after** apply/read | error, blocks dependents |\n| `check` block | top-level | end of plan & apply | **warning only**, doesn't block |\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  lifecycle {\n    precondition {\n      condition     = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.architecture == \"x86_64\"\n      error_message = \"AMI must be x86_64.\"\n    }\n    postcondition {\n      condition     = self.public_dns != \"\"      # `self` only in postconditions\n      error_message = \"Instance must have a public DNS name.\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\ncheck \"health\" {\n  data \"http\" \"site\" { url = \"https://example.com\" }     # scoped data source\n  assert {\n    condition     = data.http.site.status_code == 200\n    error_message = \"Site is not healthy.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 7 — Sensitive data & secrets (Vault)\n\n- `sensitive = true` on variables/outputs **redacts CLI output** only. Values are **still in state and\n  plan files in plain text**.\n- `ephemeral = true` variables/outputs and **ephemeral resources** (`ephemeral \"...\" \"...\" {}`, Terraform 1.10+)\n  are **never written to state or plan**. **Write-only arguments** (`*_wo`, 1.11+) let you pass secrets to a\n  provider without persisting them.\n- Protect state: remote backend with **encryption at rest** + strict **access control**; never commit\n  `*.tfstate` or secret `*.tfvars` to Git.\n- Never hard-code credentials in `.tf` files. Use env vars, IAM roles / workload identity, or a secrets manager.\n- **HashiCorp Vault** integration:\n  - `vault` provider → read secrets with data sources (e.g. `data \"vault_kv_secret_v2\"`) or, better,\n    ephemeral resources, at run time.\n  - **Dynamic secrets**: Vault generates short-lived cloud credentials (e.g. AWS keys with a TTL) for each\n    Terraform run, reducing blast radius.\n  - HCP Terraform supports **dynamic provider credentials** (OIDC workload identity) with AWS/Azure/GCP/Vault —\n    no static credentials stored at all.\n- In HCP Terraform, mark workspace variables as **sensitive** (write-only in the UI/API).\n\n---\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `resource` manages, `data` reads.\n- Variable precedence: default < env < tfvars < tfvars.json < auto.tfvars < CLI.\n- `for_each` > `count` for non-identical instances. `dynamic` for nested blocks.\n- Implicit dependencies via references; `depends_on` only for hidden ones.\n- `validation` / `pre` / `postcondition` block runs; `check` only warns.\n- `sensitive` ≠ encrypted: it's still in state.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. How do you reference the `id` of a data source `aws_vpc.main`?\n2. A variable is set by `default`, `TF_VAR_`, `terraform.tfvars` and `-var`. Which wins?\n3. What's the difference between `list` and `set`?\n4. Which meta-argument would you use to create 3 users named alice, bob, carol with stable addresses?\n5. When should you use `depends_on`?\n6. What does `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }` do?\n7. Which custom condition type produces only a warning?\n8. Does `sensitive = true` keep a value out of the state file?\n9. What's the name of the iterator in `dynamic \"setting\" { ... }`?\n10. Can you write your own functions in HCL?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `data.aws_vpc.main.id`.\n2. `-var` on the command line.\n3. Lists are ordered and allow duplicates; sets are unordered, unique, and have no index access.\n4. `for_each = toset([\"alice\",\"bob\",\"carol\"])`.\n5. Only for hidden dependencies Terraform can't infer from references.\n6. Creates the replacement before destroying the old one (avoids downtime).\n7. `check` blocks.\n8. **No** — only redacts output. Use `ephemeral` or write-only args to avoid persisting.\n9. `setting` (unless renamed with `iterator`).\n10. No — only built-in functions (plus provider-defined functions).\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 04 — Variables, types, expressions, meta-arguments, conditions, sensitive data\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/04-terraform-configuration/lab\nterraform init\nterraform apply\n```\n\nExplore the `output/` directory and `terraform state list`.\n\n## Exercise 1 — Resource vs data source\n- Which addresses in `terraform state list` start with `data.`? Why is it in state if it's not managed?\n  (Data sources are cached in state, but Terraform never creates/destroys the underlying object.)\n\n## Exercise 2 — Variable precedence\n```bash\nterraform plan -var 'environment=prod'                       # CLI flag\nTF_VAR_environment=stage terraform plan                       # env var\ncp example.tfvars terraform.tfvars && terraform plan          # auto-loaded tfvars\nTF_VAR_environment=dev terraform plan -var 'environment=prod' # who wins? (CLI)\nrm terraform.tfvars\n```\nTry an invalid value: `terraform plan -var 'environment=qa'` → validation error.\n\n## Exercise 3 — count vs for_each\n1. Apply with `-var 'admins=[\"alice\",\"bob\",\"carol\"]'`.\n2. Now remove **bob** (the middle one): `-var 'admins=[\"alice\",\"carol\"]'` and **plan**.\n   Notice that `local_file.admin[1]` is **replaced** (index shift) and `[2]` destroyed.\n3. Remove a server from `var.servers` instead (edit the default). Only that one key is destroyed — stable addresses.\n\n## Exercise 4 — Expressions in the console\n```bash\nterraform console\n> local.common_tags\n> { for k, v in var.servers : k => v.size }\n> [for n in var.admins : upper(n) if n != \"bob\"]\n> local_file.admin[*].filename\n> cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 3)\n> lookup(var.tags, \"owner\", \"unknown\")\n> try(var.servers[\"db\"].size, \"no db\")\n> var.db_password           # (sensitive)\n```\n\n## Exercise 5 — Custom conditions\n1. Set a server size to `\"huge\"` in `example.tfvars` and plan with `-var-file=example.tfvars` → **precondition** fails.\n2. The `check \"password_strength\"` block shows a **warning** on every plan but never blocks. Set\n   `TF_VAR_db_password=a-very-long-and-strong-secret` and plan again — warning gone.\n\n## Exercise 6 — Sensitive data\n```bash\nterraform output                 # db_password = <sensitive>\nterraform output -raw db_password\ngrep -A2 db_password terraform.tfstate   # plain text in state!\n```\nTakeaway: `sensitive` only redacts CLI output — protect your state.\n\n## Exercise 7 — lifecycle\n- Change `project` to `acme2` and plan. `random_id.suffix` is replaced (keepers changed), so every server file\n  changes. Because of `create_before_destroy`, the plan shows `+/-` for the server files.\n- Add `prevent_destroy = true` to `random_id.suffix`, then run `terraform plan -destroy` → error. Remove it.\n\n## Exercise 8 — Dependencies\n```bash\nterraform graph | grep -E 'inventory|admin'\n```\n`terraform_data.inventory` depends on `local_file.admin` explicitly (`depends_on`) and on `random_id.suffix` implicitly.\n\n## Clean up\n```bash\nterraform destroy\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "example.tfvars", "content": "# Copy to terraform.tfvars (auto-loaded) or pass with -var-file=example.tfvars\nproject     = \"shop\"\nenvironment = \"stage\"\n\nservers = {\n  web    = { size = \"small\", ports = [80, 443] }\n  api    = { size = \"large\", replicas = 3, ports = [8080, 8443] }\n  worker = { size = \"medium\" }\n}\n\nadmins = [\"alice\", \"bob\", \"carol\"]\n\ntags = {\n  owner = \"platform-team\"\n}\n"}, {"path": "main.tf", "content": "locals {\n  name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${var.environment}\"\n\n  common_tags = merge(\n    {\n      project     = var.project\n      environment = var.environment\n      managed_by  = \"terraform\"\n    },\n    var.tags,\n  )\n\n  # for expression producing a map, with a filter\n  multi_replica_servers = { for name, s in var.servers : name => s.replicas if s.replicas > 1 }\n\n  # conditional expression\n  log_level = var.environment == \"prod\" ? \"warn\" : \"debug\"\n}\n\n# Suffix to make names unique. Changing `keepers` forces a new ID.\nresource \"random_id\" \"suffix\" {\n  byte_length = 3\n  keepers = {\n    project = var.project\n  }\n}\n\n# for_each over a map: one file per server, stable addresses like local_file.server[\"web\"]\nresource \"local_file\" \"server\" {\n  for_each = var.servers\n\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/${local.name_prefix}-${each.key}.conf\"\n  content = templatefile(\"${path.module}/templates/server.conf.tftpl\", {\n    name      = \"${local.name_prefix}-${each.key}-${random_id.suffix.hex}\" # implicit dependency on random_id\n    size      = each.value.size\n    replicas  = each.value.replicas\n    ports     = each.value.ports\n    log_level = local.log_level\n    tags      = local.common_tags\n  })\n\n  lifecycle {\n    create_before_destroy = true\n\n    precondition {\n      condition     = contains([\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\"], each.value.size)\n      error_message = \"Server ${each.key}: size must be small, medium or large.\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# count over a list: local_file.admin[0], local_file.admin[1] ...\nresource \"local_file\" \"admin\" {\n  count = length(var.admins)\n\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/admins/${count.index}-${var.admins[count.index]}.txt\"\n  content  = \"admin=${var.admins[count.index]}\\n\"\n}\n\n# Conditional creation with count = condition ? 1 : 0\nresource \"local_file\" \"prod_banner\" {\n  count = var.environment == \"prod\" ? 1 : 0\n\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/PRODUCTION\"\n  content  = \"Careful: this is production.\\n\"\n}\n\n# Sensitive value written to a file: local_sensitive_file hides content in plan output.\nresource \"local_sensitive_file\" \"db_credentials\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/db.env\"\n  content  = \"DB_PASSWORD=${var.db_password}\\n\"\n}\n\n# Explicit dependency: nothing references the admin files, but we want this to run after them.\nresource \"terraform_data\" \"inventory\" {\n  input = {\n    servers = keys(local_file.server)\n    admins  = local_file.admin[*].filename # splat expression\n  }\n\n  triggers_replace = [random_id.suffix.hex]\n\n  depends_on = [local_file.admin]\n}\n\n# Data source: read back a file we created (data sources read, never manage)\ndata \"local_file\" \"web_conf\" {\n  filename = local_file.server[\"web\"].filename\n\n  lifecycle {\n    postcondition {\n      condition     = strcontains(self.content, \"size\")\n      error_message = \"Rendered config is missing the size setting.\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# check block: produces a WARNING, never blocks the run\ncheck \"password_strength\" {\n  assert {\n    condition     = length(var.db_password) >= 16\n    error_message = \"db_password is shorter than 16 characters. Consider a stronger secret.\"\n  }\n}\n"}, {"path": "outputs.tf", "content": "output \"server_files\" {\n  description = \"Map of server name => rendered file path\"\n  value       = { for name, f in local_file.server : name => f.filename }\n}\n\noutput \"admin_files\" {\n  description = \"Admin files, via splat\"\n  value       = local_file.admin[*].filename\n}\n\noutput \"multi_replica_servers\" {\n  value = local.multi_replica_servers\n}\n\noutput \"web_conf_preview\" {\n  description = \"First line of the web config, read through a data source\"\n  value       = split(\"\\n\", data.local_file.web_conf.content)[0]\n}\n\noutput \"db_password\" {\n  description = \"Redacted in CLI output; visible with `terraform output -raw db_password` and in state!\"\n  value       = var.db_password\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n"}, {"path": "templates/server.conf.tftpl", "content": "# server: ${name}\nsize      = ${size}\nreplicas  = ${replicas}\nlog_level = ${log_level}\n\n%{ for port in ports ~}\nlisten ${port}\n%{ endfor ~}\n\n[tags]\n%{ for k, v in tags ~}\n${k} = ${v}\n%{ endfor ~}\n"}, {"path": "variables.tf", "content": "variable \"project\" {\n  description = \"Project name, used as a prefix\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"acme\"\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = can(regex(\"^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,15}$\", var.project))\n    error_message = \"project must be 3-16 chars, lowercase letters, digits or dashes, starting with a letter.\"\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"environment\" {\n  description = \"Deployment environment\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"dev\"\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"dev\", \"stage\", \"prod\"], var.environment)\n    error_message = \"environment must be one of: dev, stage, prod.\"\n  }\n}\n\n# Complex type: map of objects with an optional attribute + default\nvariable \"servers\" {\n  description = \"Servers to 'provision' (rendered as config files)\"\n  type = map(object({\n    size     = string\n    replicas = optional(number, 1)\n    ports    = optional(list(number), [80])\n  }))\n  default = {\n    web = { size = \"small\", ports = [80, 443] }\n    api = { size = \"medium\", replicas = 2, ports = [8080] }\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"admins\" {\n  description = \"Admin usernames (demonstrates count)\"\n  type        = list(string)\n  default     = [\"alice\", \"bob\"]\n}\n\nvariable \"tags\" {\n  description = \"Extra tags merged into the common tags\"\n  type        = map(string)\n  default     = {}\n}\n\nvariable \"db_password\" {\n  description = \"Demo secret. Set with TF_VAR_db_password. Redacted in output but STILL stored in state.\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n  default     = \"changeme\"\n}\n"}, {"path": "versions.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "05-modules", "num": "05", "title": "Terraform Modules", "dir": "lessons/05-modules", "md": "# Lesson 05 — Terraform Modules\n\n**Objectives covered:** 5a–5d · **Time:** ~1.5 h\n\n## 1. What is a module?\n\nA module is **a set of `.tf` files in a single directory**. That's it.\n\n- **Root module** — the directory where you run `terraform`. Every configuration has one.\n- **Child module** — a module called by another module via a `module` block.\n- **Published modules** — shared via the public Terraform Registry or a private registry.\n\nWhy: reuse, encapsulation, consistency, standardization of best practices, less copy-paste.\n\n## 2. Calling a module\n\n```hcl\nmodule \"web_bucket\" {\n  source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/s3-bucket/aws\"   # registry: <NAMESPACE>/<NAME>/<PROVIDER>\n  version = \"~> 4.0\"                                # only for registry modules\n\n  bucket = \"my-web-bucket\"                          # input variables of the module\n\n  # meta-arguments also work on modules\n  count      = 1              # or for_each\n  depends_on = [aws_iam_role.x]\n  providers  = { aws = aws.west }\n}\n\noutput \"bucket_arn\" {\n  value = module.web_bucket.s3_bucket_arn           # module.<NAME>.<OUTPUT>\n}\n```\n\nAfter adding or changing a module block's source/version you must run `terraform init` (or `terraform get`).\nModules are downloaded to `.terraform/modules/`.\n\n## 3. Module sources\n\n| Source | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Local path (must start with `./` or `../`) | `source = \"./modules/network\"` |\n| Terraform Registry (public) | `source = \"hashicorp/consul/aws\"` |\n| Private registry (HCP Terraform) | `source = \"app.terraform.io/my-org/vpc/aws\"` |\n| GitHub | `source = \"github.com/org/repo//modules/vpc?ref=v1.2.0\"` |\n| Generic Git | `source = \"git::https://example.com/vpc.git?ref=v1.2.0\"` |\n| Bitbucket, Mercurial (`hg::`) | `source = \"bitbucket.org/org/repo\"` |\n| HTTP URL (archive) | `source = \"https://example.com/vpc-module.zip\"` |\n| S3 / GCS buckets | `source = \"s3::https://s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/bucket/vpc.zip\"` |\n\nNotes:\n- `//` separates the repo from a **subdirectory**; `?ref=` selects a Git branch/tag/commit.\n- Local path modules are **not downloaded/copied** — referenced directly, and they share the repo's version.\n- The `version` argument **only works for registry sources** (public or private). For Git, pin with `?ref=`.\n\n## 4. Public registry module requirements (for publishing)\n\n- Hosted on **GitHub** (public repo), named `terraform-<PROVIDER>-<NAME>` (e.g. `terraform-aws-vpc`).\n- Standard module structure: `README.md`, `main.tf`, `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf`, (`examples/`, `modules/`).\n- Versions published from **semantic version tags** (`v1.0.0` or `1.0.0`).\n- Registry modules can be marked verified/partner.\n\n## 5. Variable scope — inputs and outputs are the only interface\n\n- A child module **cannot** see the parent's variables, locals, or resources. Everything must be passed in as\n  **input variables**.\n- The parent **cannot** read a child's resources directly — only what the child exposes as **outputs**\n  (`module.name.output_name`).\n- Locals are private to the module that defines them.\n- Providers: child modules **inherit** the default (un-aliased) provider configurations from the parent.\n  Aliased configs must be passed explicitly with `providers = { ... }`. Modules should declare\n  `required_providers` but generally **should not** contain `provider` blocks.\n\n```\nroot module                        child module ./modules/app\n─────────────                      ──────────────────────────\nvar.env ───── env = var.env ─────▶ variable \"env\" {}\n                                   resource \"x\" \"y\" { ... }\nmodule.app.url ◀──────────────── output \"url\" { value = x.y.url }\n```\n\n## 6. Module versioning\n\n- Always pin registry modules: `version = \"~> 4.0\"` or exact `\"4.1.2\"`.\n- `terraform init` installs the newest version matching the constraint *if nothing installed*;\n  `terraform init -upgrade` checks for newer matching versions.\n- Module versions are **not recorded in `.terraform.lock.hcl`** (providers only) — so pinning in code matters.\n- For Git sources use tags: `?ref=v1.2.0` (or a commit SHA for immutability).\n\n## 7. Module best practices\n\n- Keep modules small and focused; avoid thin wrappers around a single resource.\n- Expose useful outputs; document variables with `description` and `type`.\n- Standard structure: `main.tf`, `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf`, `versions.tf`, `README.md`.\n- Nested modules go in `modules/`; examples in `examples/`.\n- Use `moved` blocks when refactoring resources into modules (Lesson 06).\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Module = a directory of `.tf` files; root module is where you run Terraform.\n- Communication: **inputs in, outputs out**. No implicit access to parent scope.\n- `version` only for registry modules; Git uses `?ref=`.\n- Run `terraform init` after adding/changing modules.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. What's the minimum requirement for something to be a Terraform module?\n2. How does a parent read a value from a child module named `network`?\n3. Can a child module read the parent's `var.region` automatically?\n4. You pin a GitHub module source with `version = \"1.2.0\"`. Does it work?\n5. Which command must you run after adding a new module block?\n6. Registry source format?\n7. Naming format for a public registry module repo?\n8. Are module versions tracked in `.terraform.lock.hcl`?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. A directory containing `.tf` files.\n2. Via an output: `module.network.<output_name>`.\n3. No — it must be passed as an input variable.\n4. No — `version` is only for registry sources; use `?ref=v1.2.0`.\n5. `terraform init` (or `terraform get`).\n6. `<NAMESPACE>/<NAME>/<PROVIDER>` (with optional hostname prefix for private registries).\n7. `terraform-<PROVIDER>-<NAME>`.\n8. No.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 05 — Build and consume a module\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/05-modules/lab\nterraform init        # note \"Initializing modules...\"\ncat .terraform/modules/modules.json\nterraform apply\nterraform state list\n```\n\nAddresses look like `module.docs_site.local_file.page[\"index.html\"]` and\n`module.marketing[\"blog\"].random_id.build`.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. **Scope:** In the root `main.tf`, add `output \"x\" { value = module.docs_site.local.site_dir }`\n   and run `terraform validate`. Why does it fail? Fix it by using the `site_dir` output instead.\n2. **Inputs:** Remove the `environment = var.environment` line from `module \"docs_site\"` and plan.\n   The module falls back to its own default (`dev`) — it does *not* see the root variable.\n   Run `terraform plan -var environment=prod` to see which sites change. Restore the line.\n3. **New module call:** Add a `module \"status_page\"` block and run `terraform plan` *before* `init`.\n   What error do you get? Run `terraform init` (or `terraform get`) and plan again.\n4. **Add a page to one marketing site** by editing `local.marketing_sites`. Only that instance changes.\n5. **Refactor with `moved`:** rename `module \"docs_site\"` to `module \"documentation\"`. Plan: everything\n   is destroyed/recreated. Now add:\n   ```hcl\n   moved {\n     from = module.docs_site\n     to   = module.documentation\n   }\n   ```\n   Plan again → no destroy, just moves. (More in Lesson 06.)\n6. **Bonus (needs internet):** convert the module into a Git repo, tag `v1.0.0`, and call it with\n   `source = \"git::file:///ABSOLUTE/PATH/static-site?ref=v1.0.0\"`.\n\n## Clean up\n```bash\nterraform destroy\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"environment\" {\n  type    = string\n  default = \"dev\"\n}\n\n# 1) A single module call with a local path source\nmodule \"docs_site\" {\n  source = \"./modules/static-site\"\n\n  site_name   = \"docs\"\n  base_dir    = \"${path.root}/output\"\n  environment = var.environment # passed explicitly: modules can't see the parent's variables\n  pages = {\n    \"index.html\"   = \"Documentation\"\n    \"install.html\" = \"Installation\"\n  }\n}\n\n# 2) The same module instantiated many times with for_each\nlocals {\n  marketing_sites = {\n    blog  = { \"index.html\" = \"Blog\", \"about.html\" = \"About us\" }\n    store = { \"index.html\" = \"Store\" }\n  }\n}\n\nmodule \"marketing\" {\n  source   = \"./modules/static-site\"\n  for_each = local.marketing_sites\n\n  site_name   = each.key\n  base_dir    = \"${path.root}/output\"\n  pages       = each.value\n  environment = var.environment\n}\n\n# 3) Registry module example (commented out - needs a cloud account):\n# module \"vpc\" {\n#   source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\"\n#   version = \"~> 6.0\"\n#   name    = \"demo\"\n#   cidr    = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n# }\n\noutput \"docs_pages\" {\n  value = module.docs_site.page_paths\n}\n\noutput \"marketing_dirs\" {\n  value = { for name, m in module.marketing : name => m.site_dir }\n}\n"}, {"path": "modules/static-site/README.md", "content": "# static-site module\n\nRenders a tiny static website to the local filesystem.\n\n## Inputs\n\n| Name | Type | Default | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `site_name` | `string` | — (required) | Folder name for the site |\n| `base_dir` | `string` | — (required) | Where to render |\n| `pages` | `map(string)` | `{ \"index.html\" = \"Home\" }` | file name => title |\n| `environment` | `string` | `\"dev\"` | Footer label |\n\n## Outputs\n\n| Name | Description |\n|---|---|\n| `site_dir` | Rendered directory |\n| `page_paths` | List of page files |\n| `build_id` | Random build id |\n"}, {"path": "modules/static-site/main.tf", "content": "locals {\n  # Private to this module: the root module cannot read local.site_dir.\n  site_dir = \"${var.base_dir}/${var.site_name}\"\n}\n\nresource \"random_id\" \"build\" {\n  byte_length = 4\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"page\" {\n  for_each = var.pages\n\n  filename = \"${local.site_dir}/${each.key}\"\n  content  = <<-HTML\n    <!doctype html>\n    <html>\n      <head><title>${each.value} | ${var.site_name}</title></head>\n      <body>\n        <h1>${each.value}</h1>\n        <footer>env=${var.environment} build=${random_id.build.hex}</footer>\n      </body>\n    </html>\n  HTML\n}\n"}, {"path": "modules/static-site/outputs.tf", "content": "output \"site_dir\" {\n  description = \"Directory the site was rendered to\"\n  value       = local.site_dir\n}\n\noutput \"page_paths\" {\n  description = \"Paths of all rendered pages\"\n  value       = [for p in local_file.page : p.filename]\n}\n\noutput \"build_id\" {\n  description = \"Random build identifier\"\n  value       = random_id.build.hex\n}\n"}, {"path": "modules/static-site/variables.tf", "content": "variable \"site_name\" {\n  description = \"Name of the site; used as the folder name\"\n  type        = string\n}\n\nvariable \"base_dir\" {\n  description = \"Directory where the site is rendered\"\n  type        = string\n}\n\nvariable \"pages\" {\n  description = \"Map of page file name => page title\"\n  type        = map(string)\n  default = {\n    \"index.html\" = \"Home\"\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"environment\" {\n  description = \"Environment label shown in the footer\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"dev\"\n}\n"}, {"path": "modules/static-site/versions.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  # Modules declare required providers but do NOT configure them (no provider blocks).\n  required_providers {\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \">= 2.5\"\n    }\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \">= 3.6\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "06-state-management", "num": "06", "title": "Terraform State Management", "dir": "lessons/06-state-management", "md": "# Lesson 06 — Terraform State Management\n\n**Objectives covered:** 6a–6d · **Time:** ~2 h\n\n## 1. Backends\n\nA **backend** defines **where state is stored** (and, for some, where locking happens).\n\n### 1.1 The local backend (default)\n- Used when no `backend` block or `cloud` block exists.\n- State stored in `terraform.tfstate` in the root module directory; previous state in `terraform.tfstate.backup`.\n- Locks using OS-level file locking on the local system.\n- Fine for learning / solo work; bad for teams (no sharing, easy to lose, secrets on laptops).\n- Can be configured explicitly:\n  ```hcl\n  terraform {\n    backend \"local\" {\n      path = \"state/dev.tfstate\"\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n- With CLI workspaces, non-default workspace states live in `terraform.tfstate.d/<workspace>/terraform.tfstate`.\n\n### 1.2 Remote backends\nCommon: `s3`, `azurerm`, `gcs`, `consul`, `kubernetes`, `pg`, `http`, `remote` (legacy, HCP Terraform), plus the\n`cloud` block for HCP Terraform (preferred for HCP).\n\nBenefits: **shared** state for teams, **locking**, **encryption at rest**, versioning/backups,\nsecrets not on laptops.\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket       = \"acme-terraform-state\"\n    key          = \"network/prod/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region       = \"eu-west-1\"\n    encrypt      = true\n    use_lockfile = true          # S3-native locking (DynamoDB locking is deprecated)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRules:\n- Only **one** backend block per configuration.\n- Backend blocks **cannot use variables, locals, or data source references** (evaluated before everything else).\n- Changing the backend requires `terraform init`; Terraform offers to **migrate** existing state\n  (`-migrate-state`), or `-reconfigure` to ignore the existing one.\n- **Partial configuration**: leave out sensitive/dynamic settings and supply them at init:\n  ```bash\n  terraform init -backend-config=\"bucket=acme-state\" -backend-config=backend.hcl\n  ```\n  Credentials should come from env vars or credential files, not the backend block (backend config is stored in\n  `.terraform/` and in plan files).\n\n### 1.3 Reading another configuration's state\n```hcl\ndata \"terraform_remote_state\" \"network\" {\n  backend = \"s3\"\n  config = {\n    bucket = \"acme-terraform-state\"\n    key    = \"network/prod/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region = \"eu-west-1\"\n  }\n}\n# data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id   (only ROOT-module OUTPUTS are accessible)\n```\nIn HCP Terraform, prefer the `tfe_outputs` data source. Note that access to remote state requires access to the whole\nstate snapshot, which may include secrets.\n\n## 2. State locking\n\n- Prevents concurrent operations from corrupting state (two `apply`s at once).\n- Happens **automatically** for any operation that could write state (`plan`, `apply`, `destroy`, `state` cmds…) —\n  *if the backend supports locking* (local, s3, azurerm, gcs, consul, HCP Terraform… do).\n- If locking fails, Terraform stops (doesn't continue).\n- `-lock=false` disables locking (dangerous). `-lock-timeout=60s` waits for a lock.\n- A stuck lock (e.g. crashed process) → `terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID>`. Only use when you're sure no one else\n  is running; it doesn't touch the other process. Only needed/usable for backends where locking is supported.\n\n## 3. Inspecting and modifying state (CLI)\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `terraform state list` | List resource addresses in state (supports filter: `state list module.vpc`) |\n| `terraform state show ADDR` | Show attributes of one resource |\n| `terraform show` | Show the whole state (or a saved plan) in human form; `-json` for machines |\n| `terraform state mv SRC DST` | Rename/move a resource address in state (prefer `moved` block) |\n| `terraform state rm ADDR` | Stop managing a resource **without destroying it** (prefer `removed` block) |\n| `terraform state pull` | Output the current (remote) state as JSON to stdout |\n| `terraform state push FILE` | Overwrite remote state with a local file (dangerous; protects against lineage/serial mismatches unless `-force`) |\n| `terraform state replace-provider A B` | Change the provider source of resources in state |\n| `terraform import ADDR ID` | Import existing object into state (prefer `import` block — Lesson 07) |\n| `terraform output` | Read root outputs from state |\n\n`state` subcommands that modify state write a backup automatically (for the local backend).\n\n## 4. Config-driven refactoring: `moved` and `removed` blocks\n\n```hcl\n# Rename without destroy/recreate\nmoved {\n  from = aws_instance.web\n  to   = aws_instance.app\n}\n\n# Also for count → for_each and into modules\nmoved {\n  from = aws_instance.app[0]\n  to   = aws_instance.app[\"primary\"]\n}\nmoved {\n  from = aws_s3_bucket.logs\n  to   = module.logging.aws_s3_bucket.this\n}\n\n# Stop managing a resource but keep the real object (1.7+)\nremoved {\n  from = aws_instance.legacy\n  lifecycle {\n    destroy = false\n  }\n}\n```\nAdvantages over `state mv` / `state rm`: reviewable in PRs, visible in `plan`, works in HCP Terraform\nand for module consumers.\n\n## 5. Drift\n\n**Drift** = real infrastructure changed outside Terraform (console clicks, other tools, deleted resources).\n\nHow Terraform handles it:\n- Every `plan`/`apply` **refreshes** by default → detects drift and plans to bring reality back to the config.\n- `terraform plan -refresh-only` → shows how **state** would be updated to match reality, with no infra changes.\n- `terraform apply -refresh-only` → accepts the real-world changes into state (replaces the deprecated\n  `terraform refresh` command, which applies without review).\n- After a refresh-only apply, update your config to match if you want to keep the change; otherwise the next normal\n  apply reverts it.\n- `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [...] }` for attributes legitimately managed elsewhere (e.g. autoscaling `desired_count`).\n- If the object was **deleted** outside Terraform, refresh removes it from state and the plan recreates it.\n- HCP Terraform has **health assessments / drift detection** that check periodically (Lesson 08).\n\n### Forcing replacement\n- `terraform apply -replace=\"aws_instance.web\"` — preferred; shows in plan.\n- `terraform taint` / `untaint` — **deprecated** in favour of `-replace`.\n\n## 6. State file facts to memorize\n\n- Format: JSON. Contains `version`, `terraform_version`, `serial` (increments each write), `lineage`\n  (unique ID of the state's history), `outputs`, `resources`.\n- **May contain secrets in plain text** → encrypt at rest, restrict access, never commit to VCS.\n- Terraform needs state to work; it is **not** optional. Don't edit manually.\n- Workspaces (CLI): `terraform workspace new|select|list|show|delete` — multiple state files for **one**\n  configuration and backend. `default` workspace always exists and can't be deleted.\n  `terraform.workspace` returns the current name. Not suitable for strong isolation (same backend/credentials).\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Default = local backend, `terraform.tfstate`.\n- Remote backends: share + lock + encrypt. No variables in backend blocks. Change backend → `init`.\n- Locking is automatic where supported; `force-unlock` for stuck locks.\n- `-refresh-only` for drift; `-replace` instead of `taint`; `moved`/`removed` instead of `state mv`/`rm`.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. Where does the local backend store state by default?\n2. Can you use `var.bucket_name` inside a `backend \"s3\"` block?\n3. What happens after you change the backend block and run `terraform plan` without `init`?\n4. Which command releases a stuck lock?\n5. What's the safe way to accept manual changes into state without modifying infrastructure?\n6. How do you stop managing a resource without destroying it (two ways)?\n7. What replaced `terraform taint`?\n8. Which values from another state can `terraform_remote_state` read?\n9. Does `terraform state rm` delete the real resource?\n10. Which state file field increments on every write?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `terraform.tfstate` in the root module (working) directory.\n2. No — backend config can't reference variables; use partial configuration with `-backend-config`.\n3. Error: backend configuration changed, run `terraform init` (with `-migrate-state` or `-reconfigure`).\n4. `terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID`.\n5. `terraform apply -refresh-only`.\n6. `removed { from = ... lifecycle { destroy = false } }` block, or `terraform state rm`.\n7. `terraform apply -replace=ADDR`.\n8. Only the root module outputs.\n9. No.\n10. `serial`.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 06 — Backends, locking, state commands, drift, refactoring\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/06-state-management/lab\nterraform init\nterraform apply -auto-approve\n```\n\n## Exercise 1 — Local backend & migration\n1. `ls` → `terraform.tfstate` is here (default local backend).\n2. Uncomment the `backend \"local\"` block in `main.tf`, then `terraform plan` → error: backend changed.\n3. `terraform init -migrate-state` → answer `yes`. State now lives in `state/terraform.tfstate`.\n4. Re-comment the block and `terraform init -migrate-state` to move it back.\n\n## Exercise 2 — Inspect state\n```bash\nterraform state list\nterraform state show random_pet.app\nterraform show\nterraform state pull | head -20       # note serial and lineage\nterraform output -json\n```\n\n## Exercise 3 — Locking\nIn terminal 1: `terraform apply` (**don't** answer the prompt; leave it waiting).\nIn terminal 2: `terraform plan` → `Error acquiring the state lock`. Note the lock ID.\nTry `terraform plan -lock-timeout=10s`. Then answer `no` in terminal 1.\n> `terraform force-unlock <ID>` is only for **stale** locks left by crashed processes.\n\n## Exercise 4 — Drift & refresh-only\n```bash\necho \"manually edited\" > output/app.conf       # simulate an out-of-band change\nterraform plan -refresh-only                    # shows what changed in reality\nterraform plan                                  # plans to restore the file to config\nrm output/legacy.txt                            # simulate deletion outside Terraform\nterraform plan                                  # plans to re-create it\nterraform apply -auto-approve                   # back to desired state\n```\n\n## Exercise 5 — Force replacement\n```bash\nterraform plan -replace=random_pet.app\nterraform apply -replace=random_pet.app -auto-approve\n```\n\n## Exercise 6 — Refactor with `moved`\nRename `resource \"random_pet\" \"app\"` to `resource \"random_pet\" \"application\"` (also fix the references).\n`terraform plan` → destroy + create. Now add:\n```hcl\nmoved {\n  from = random_pet.app\n  to   = random_pet.application\n}\n```\n`terraform plan` → `has moved to`, no changes. Apply. (Equivalent CLI: `terraform state mv`.)\n\n## Exercise 7 — Stop managing with `removed`\nDelete the `local_file.legacy` resource block and add:\n```hcl\nremoved {\n  from = local_file.legacy\n  lifecycle {\n    destroy = false\n  }\n}\n```\n`terraform apply` → it's removed from state but `output/legacy.txt` still exists.\n(Equivalent CLI: `terraform state rm local_file.legacy`.)\n\n## Exercise 8 — CLI workspaces\n```bash\nterraform workspace list\nterraform workspace new staging\nterraform apply -auto-approve         # a brand-new state: everything is created again\ncat output/app.conf                   # workspace=staging\nls terraform.tfstate.d/\nterraform workspace select default\nterraform workspace delete -force staging   # -force because its state isn't empty\n```\n(Note both workspaces wrote to the same file path — a hint for why you'd use\n`terraform.workspace` in names.)\n\n## Clean up\n```bash\nterraform destroy -auto-approve\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n  }\n\n  # Exercise 1: uncomment, then run `terraform init -migrate-state`\n  # backend \"local\" {\n  #   path = \"state/terraform.tfstate\"\n  # }\n}\n\nresource \"random_pet\" \"app\" {\n  length = 2\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"config\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/app.conf\"\n  content  = \"app_name=${random_pet.app.id}\\nworkspace=${terraform.workspace}\\n\"\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"legacy\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/legacy.txt\"\n  content  = \"I will be 'forgotten' by Terraform but not deleted.\\n\"\n}\n\noutput \"app_name\" {\n  value = random_pet.app.id\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "07-maintain-infrastructure", "num": "07", "title": "Maintaining Infrastructure with Terraform", "dir": "lessons/07-maintain-infrastructure", "md": "# Lesson 07 — Maintaining Infrastructure with Terraform\n\n**Objectives covered:** 7a–7c · **Time:** ~1.5 h\n\n## 1. Importing existing infrastructure\n\nImport brings an existing real-world object under Terraform management **by adding it to state**.\n\n### 1.1 `import` block (config-driven, Terraform 1.5+) — preferred\n\n```hcl\nimport {\n  to = aws_instance.web          # resource address in your config\n  id = \"i-0abc123def456\"         # provider-specific ID of the existing object\n}\n\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  # ... config matching the real object\n}\n```\n\n- Import happens during **`plan` / `apply`** — reviewable in the plan (`will be imported`), works in CI and HCP Terraform.\n- Can import many resources at once; supports `for_each` in import blocks (1.7+).\n- **Generate the config automatically:**\n  ```bash\n  terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf\n  ```\n  Terraform writes resource blocks for import targets that have no config yet. Review and clean up the output\n  (it's a starting point, may contain conflicting/default attributes).\n- After a successful apply the `import` block can be removed (or kept as a record — it's a no-op).\n\n### 1.2 `terraform import` command (legacy CLI)\n\n```bash\nterraform import aws_instance.web i-0abc123def456\nterraform import 'aws_instance.web[\"a\"]' i-0abc123def456     # quote for for_each keys\nterraform import module.app.aws_instance.web i-0abc123def456\n```\n- You must **write the resource block first** — the command does not generate config.\n- Imports **one resource at a time**, writes straight to state, no plan preview.\n- After importing, run `terraform plan` to check the config matches reality (aim for \"No changes\").\n\nCommon to both: import only affects **state**; Terraform does **not** create or modify the real object during\nimport. Not all resource types support import (check provider docs for the ID format).\n\n## 2. Using the CLI to inspect state\n\n| Question | Command |\n|---|---|\n| What resources are managed? | `terraform state list` |\n| All attributes of one resource? | `terraform state show 'aws_instance.web'` |\n| Whole state, human-readable? | `terraform show` |\n| Machine-readable state/plan? | `terraform show -json [planfile]` |\n| Root module outputs? | `terraform output`, `terraform output -json`, `terraform output -raw name` |\n| Evaluate expressions against state? | `terraform console` |\n| Raw state JSON (remote too)? | `terraform state pull` |\n| What providers are required? | `terraform providers` |\n| Which version of core/providers? | `terraform version` |\n| Dependency graph? | `terraform graph` |\n\n## 3. Verbose logging and debugging\n\nTerraform logs are controlled by environment variables:\n\n| Variable | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `TF_LOG` | Log level: **`TRACE`** (most verbose), `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARN`, `ERROR`, or `OFF`. `JSON` = TRACE in JSON format |\n| `TF_LOG_CORE` | Level for Terraform core only |\n| `TF_LOG_PROVIDER` | Level for providers only |\n| `TF_LOG_PATH` | Write logs to this file (**requires `TF_LOG` to be set**) |\n\n```bash\nexport TF_LOG=TRACE\nexport TF_LOG_PATH=./terraform-debug.log\nterraform apply\nunset TF_LOG TF_LOG_PATH\n```\n\n- Logs go to **stderr** by default.\n- Any non-empty value other than a valid level is treated as `TRACE`.\n- If Terraform **crashes** (panics), it writes `crash.log` with the stack trace — attach it when filing a bug.\n- Useful when: provider/API errors are unclear, auth problems, slow operations, reporting bugs.\n- Logs may contain secrets — don't share them blindly.\n\n## 4. Other maintenance tasks\n\n- **Upgrading providers:** bump the constraint → `terraform init -upgrade` → `plan` → commit the lock file.\n- **Upgrading Terraform:** read the upgrade guide, update `required_version`, run plan, fix deprecations.\n  Terraform 1.x has a compatibility promise for the language and state within 1.x.\n- **Replacing a broken resource:** `terraform apply -replace=ADDR`.\n- **Targeting:** `-target=ADDR` only for exceptional recovery; not routine use.\n- **Testing:** `terraform test` with `*.tftest.hcl` files (`run` blocks with `command = plan|apply` and `assert`s).\n- **Formatting/validation in CI:** `terraform fmt -check -recursive`, `terraform validate`.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Prefer `import` blocks + `plan -generate-config-out`; the `terraform import` CLI needs config written first.\n- Import changes state only.\n- `TF_LOG=TRACE` most verbose; `TF_LOG_PATH` needs `TF_LOG`.\n- Crash → `crash.log`.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. Which flag generates HCL for resources in `import` blocks?\n2. Does `terraform import` (CLI) write the resource configuration for you?\n3. What does import modify: the real resource, the state, or both?\n4. Most verbose log level?\n5. You set `TF_LOG_PATH=tf.log` but no file appears. Why?\n6. Name of the file written when Terraform panics?\n7. Which command shows all attributes of `aws_s3_bucket.logs` from state?\n8. After importing, how do you confirm your configuration matches the real object?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `terraform plan -generate-config-out=<file>.tf`.\n2. No.\n3. Only the state.\n4. `TRACE`.\n5. `TF_LOG` isn't set — `TF_LOG_PATH` has no effect without it.\n6. `crash.log`.\n7. `terraform state show aws_s3_bucket.logs`.\n8. Run `terraform plan` and expect no changes.\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 07 — Import, inspect, debug\n\n```bash\ncd lessons/07-maintain-infrastructure/lab\nterraform init\n```\n\n## Exercise 1 — `import` block + generated config\n1. Uncomment the `import { ... }` block in `main.tf`.\n2. Generate the configuration:\n   ```bash\n   terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf\n   cat generated.tf\n   ```\n3. Review `generated.tf` (it's a starting point: you may need to remove conflicting attributes).\n4. `terraform apply` → plan shows `1 to import`. Check: `terraform state show random_string.legacy_token`\n   → `result = \"LegacyToken42\"`. Terraform adopted the existing value instead of generating a new one.\n5. Run `terraform plan` → `No changes`. You can now delete the `import` block.\n\n## Exercise 2 — CLI `terraform import`\n1. Apply once so `random_id.session` exists, and note its ID (the `b64_url` attribute):\n   ```bash\n   terraform apply -auto-approve\n   terraform state show random_id.session | grep b64_url\n   ```\n2. Remove it from state (pretend it was created by hand):\n   ```bash\n   terraform state rm random_id.session\n   terraform plan          # Terraform wants to create a NEW one\n   ```\n3. Import it back using the `b64_url` value you noted:\n   ```bash\n   terraform import random_id.session <B64_URL_VALUE>\n   terraform state show random_id.session\n   terraform plan          # No changes: config, state and \"reality\" agree again\n   ```\n   Note: no plan preview during CLI import, and the config had to exist first.\n4. Try importing a *different* ID (e.g. `p-9hUg`) after another `state rm`. The plan now replaces\n   `local_file.report`, because the imported value differs and the file content depends on it.\n\n## Exercise 3 — Inspect with the CLI\n```bash\nterraform state list\nterraform show\nterraform show -json | head -c 600; echo\nterraform output -raw session_hex; echo\nterraform console <<< 'random_id.session.b64_std'\nterraform providers\nterraform version\n```\n\n## Exercise 4 — Verbose logging\n```bash\nTF_LOG_PATH=debug.log terraform plan        # no file! TF_LOG not set\nls debug.log 2>/dev/null || echo \"no log file\"\nTF_LOG=DEBUG TF_LOG_PATH=debug.log terraform plan\nwc -l debug.log\nTF_LOG=TRACE TF_LOG_PATH=trace.log terraform plan\nwc -l trace.log                               # TRACE is much more verbose\nTF_LOG_PROVIDER=TRACE terraform plan 2>&1 | head -20   # provider-only logs to stderr\nrm -f debug.log trace.log\n```\n\n## Exercise 5 — Tests\n```bash\nterraform test\n```\n\n## Clean up\n```bash\nterraform destroy -auto-approve\nrm -f generated.tf\n```\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n    local = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/local\"\n      version = \"~> 2.5\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# The random provider supports import, which lets us practice without a cloud account.\n# Pretend \"LegacyToken42\" is a value that already exists and must be adopted, not regenerated.\n\n# ---- Exercise 1: config-driven import -------------------------------------\n# Uncomment this block, then run:\n#   terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf\n#\n# import {\n#   to = random_string.legacy_token\n#   id = \"LegacyToken42\"\n# }\n\n# ---- Exercise 2: CLI import ---------------------------------------------------\n# The resource block must exist BEFORE running `terraform import`.\nresource \"random_id\" \"session\" {\n  byte_length = 4\n}\n\nresource \"local_file\" \"report\" {\n  filename = \"${path.module}/output/report.txt\"\n  content  = \"session=${random_id.session.hex}\\n\"\n}\n\noutput \"session_hex\" {\n  value = random_id.session.hex\n}\n"}, {"path": "tests/report.tftest.hcl", "content": "# Run with: terraform test\n# Each run block executes a plan or apply in an isolated, temporary state and checks assertions.\n\nrun \"report_path_is_correct\" {\n  command = plan\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = endswith(local_file.report.filename, \"output/report.txt\")\n    error_message = \"Report must be written to output/report.txt\"\n  }\n}\n\nrun \"session_id_has_expected_length\" {\n  command = apply\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = length(random_id.session.hex) == 8\n    error_message = \"4 random bytes should produce 8 hex characters\"\n  }\n}\n"}]}, {"id": "08-hcp-terraform", "num": "08", "title": "HCP Terraform", "dir": "lessons/08-hcp-terraform", "md": "# Lesson 08 — HCP Terraform\n\n**Objectives covered:** 8a–8d · **Time:** ~1.5 h\n\nHCP Terraform (formerly **Terraform Cloud**) is HashiCorp's SaaS for running Terraform as a team.\n**Terraform Enterprise** is the self-hosted distribution with the same core features.\n\n## 1. Core concepts & hierarchy\n\n```\nOrganization\n ├── Teams (permissions)\n ├── Projects  (group workspaces; permissions can be set per project)\n │    ├── Workspace: network-prod\n │    ├── Workspace: network-dev\n │    └── Workspace: app-prod\n ├── Private registry (modules & providers)\n ├── Policy sets (Sentinel / OPA)\n └── Variable sets (shared variables)\n```\n\n### Workspaces (HCP Terraform) vs CLI workspaces\n| | HCP Terraform workspace | CLI workspace (`terraform workspace`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | A full unit: config source + **variables** + **state** + run history + permissions + settings | Just an alternate **state** for the same config/backend |\n| Isolation | Separate credentials, variables, access | Same backend & credentials |\n| Typical use | One per component per environment (`network-prod`) | Quick parallel copies of one config |\n\n**Projects** group related workspaces (e.g. by team or app) and let you grant team access at the project level.\n\n## 2. Workflows (how runs are triggered)\n\n| Workflow | How it works |\n|---|---|\n| **VCS-driven** | Workspace linked to a Git repo branch. PRs trigger **speculative plans** (plan-only, can't apply); merges trigger plan + apply |\n| **CLI-driven** | You run `terraform plan/apply` locally; execution happens **remotely** in HCP Terraform, output streamed back |\n| **API-driven** | Your tooling uploads config versions and triggers runs via the API |\n\n**Execution modes:** *Remote* (default: runs on HCP Terraform's workers), *Local* (HCP Terraform stores state only;\nplans/applies run on your machine), *Agent* (runs on self-hosted **HCP Terraform agents** inside your network,\nto reach private infrastructure).\n\n## 3. Connecting the CLI — the `cloud` block\n\n```bash\nterraform login                  # opens browser, stores API token in ~/.terraform.d/credentials.tfrc.json\n```\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  cloud {\n    organization = \"my-org\"\n    # hostname = \"app.terraform.io\"        # default; set for Terraform Enterprise\n\n    workspaces {\n      name = \"app-prod\"                    # a single workspace\n      # tags = [\"app\", \"prod\"]             # or: any workspace with these tags\n      # project = \"my-project\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\nThen `terraform init`. Existing local state can be **migrated** to HCP Terraform during init.\n- You can't use both `cloud` and `backend` blocks.\n- Env vars can supply these: `TF_CLOUD_ORGANIZATION`, `TF_WORKSPACE`, `TF_CLOUD_HOSTNAME`.\n- The older `backend \"remote\"` still works but `cloud` is the preferred integration.\n\n## 4. Runs\n\nA run = plan (+ optional apply). Stages: *Pending → Plan → Cost estimation → Policy check → Apply*.\n\n- **Speculative plans**: plan-only runs (from PRs or `terraform plan` in CLI-driven workflow); can't be applied.\n- **Auto-apply** workspace setting vs manual **confirm & apply**.\n- **Run triggers**: a successful apply in workspace A can queue a run in workspace B.\n- **Run tasks**: integrate third-party tools (security scanners, cost tools) at pre-plan/post-plan/pre-apply.\n- Runs in a workspace are **queued** — only one at a time, so locking is built-in. Workspaces can be locked manually.\n\n## 5. Variables\n\n- **Terraform variables** (set `var.x`) and **environment variables** (e.g. `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `TF_LOG`).\n- Can be marked **sensitive** → write-only: never displayed again in UI/API.\n- **HCL** checkbox allows complex values.\n- **Variable sets** share variables across many workspaces/projects (e.g. cloud credentials).\n- Precedence (highest first): **priority** variable sets → workspace-specific variables →\n  project-scoped variable sets → global variable sets.\n- **Dynamic provider credentials**: OIDC-based short-lived credentials for AWS, Azure, GCP, Vault — no static secrets.\n\n## 6. Collaboration & governance\n\n| Feature | What |\n|---|---|\n| **Remote state** | Stored, encrypted, versioned, locked automatically; state history per workspace |\n| **Teams & permissions** | Org / project / workspace permissions (read, plan, write, admin; custom) |\n| **Private registry** | Share private modules and providers inside the org; supports no-code modules |\n| **Policy as code** | **Sentinel** (HashiCorp's language) and **OPA** (Rego) policies enforced between plan and apply. Enforcement levels: *advisory* (warn), *soft-mandatory* (can be overridden by authorized users), *hard-mandatory* (cannot be overridden) |\n| **Cost estimation** | Estimated monthly cost of changes for supported providers |\n| **Health assessments** | Scheduled **drift detection** and **continuous validation** (checks `check` blocks & conditions) |\n| **Audit trail** | Run history, who approved what |\n| **SSO, audit logging, agents** | Higher tiers / Enterprise |\n| **No-code provisioning** | Users create workspaces from approved no-code modules without writing HCL |\n| **Explorer** | Org-wide view of workspaces, providers, modules, versions |\n| **Ephemeral workspaces** | Auto-destroy after a set time/inactivity |\n\nSentinel/OPA policies are evaluated **after plan, before apply**.\n\n## 7. Sharing data between workspaces\n\n- `tfe_outputs` data source (tfe provider) — reads outputs of another workspace (preferred).\n- `terraform_remote_state` with `backend = \"remote\"` — requires access to the whole state.\n- Remote state sharing must be enabled for the consumer workspace (org-wide or selected workspaces).\n\n## 8. Pricing tiers (high level)\n\nFree tier is available (limited managed resources); paid tiers (Essentials/Standard/Premium) add features such as\npolicy enforcement at scale, run tasks, agents, SSO, and audit logging. Details change — check the pricing page.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `terraform login` + `cloud { organization, workspaces {} }` + `terraform init`.\n- HCP workspace = state **+ variables + permissions + runs**. CLI workspace = state only.\n- Projects group workspaces. Variable sets share variables.\n- Sentinel/OPA run between plan and apply. Hard-mandatory can't be overridden.\n- VCS PRs → speculative plans.\n\n## Review quiz\n\n1. Which command stores an API token for HCP Terraform?\n2. What block connects a configuration to HCP Terraform?\n3. What's the difference between an HCP Terraform workspace and a CLI workspace?\n4. When are Sentinel policies evaluated?\n5. Which enforcement level can never be overridden?\n6. What kind of run is triggered by a pull request in a VCS-connected workspace?\n7. Which feature lets many workspaces share the same cloud credentials variables?\n8. What lets HCP Terraform runs reach infrastructure in a private network?\n9. What groups workspaces for easier permission management?\n10. Which HCP Terraform feature periodically detects drift?\n\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n\n1. `terraform login`.\n2. `terraform { cloud { ... } }`.\n3. HCP workspace holds state, variables, permissions, settings and run history; a CLI workspace is only a separate state.\n4. After plan, before apply.\n5. Hard-mandatory.\n6. A speculative plan (plan-only).\n7. Variable sets.\n8. HCP Terraform agents (agent execution mode).\n9. Projects.\n10. Health assessments (drift detection).\n</details>\n\n➡️ Lab: [lab/LAB.md](lab/LAB.md)\n", "lab": "# Lab 08 — HCP Terraform (free tier)\n\n**Needs:** a free HCP Terraform account → <https://app.terraform.io/signup/account>.\nEverything uses the `random` provider and built-in `terraform_data`, so no cloud credentials are required.\n\n## Exercise 1 — CLI-driven workflow\n1. Create an organization in the UI (or use an existing one).\n2. Log in from the CLI:\n   ```bash\n   terraform login        # token saved to ~/.terraform.d/credentials.tfrc.json\n   ```\n3. In `main.tf`, uncomment the `cloud` block and set your organization.\n4. Initialize — the workspace is created automatically if it doesn't exist:\n   ```bash\n   cd lessons/08-hcp-terraform/lab\n   terraform init\n   ```\n5. `terraform plan` → notice the link to the run in HCP Terraform: the plan runs **remotely**.\n6. `terraform apply`. Open the workspace in the UI: check **Runs**, **States** (state versions), and outputs.\n\n## Exercise 2 — Variables & variable sets\n1. In the workspace → **Variables**, add a Terraform variable `team = data` and a **sensitive** Terraform variable\n   `api_key = s3cr3t`. Notice you can't read the sensitive value back.\n2. `terraform apply` → `deployment_name` starts with `data-`, `api_key_configured = true`.\n3. Create an organization **variable set** with an env var `TF_LOG = INFO` and apply it to the workspace.\n   Run a plan and look at the more verbose run log.\n\n## Exercise 3 — Migrating local state\n1. Comment the `cloud` block, `rm -rf .terraform`, `terraform init`, `terraform apply` (local state).\n2. Uncomment the `cloud` block (use a new workspace name) and `terraform init` → accept the prompt to\n   **migrate the local state** to HCP Terraform.\n\n## Exercise 4 — VCS-driven workflow (optional)\n1. Push this lab folder to a GitHub repo.\n2. Create a new workspace → *Version control workflow* → select the repo, set the working directory.\n3. Open a pull request that changes `length = 3` → a **speculative plan** appears as a PR check.\n4. Merge → a run is queued; confirm & apply in the UI (or enable **auto-apply** in settings).\n\n## Exercise 5 — Explore governance features (read the UI)\n- Organization settings → **Teams**, **Projects**, **Policies/Policy sets**, **Variable sets**, **Agents**.\n- Workspace settings → **Execution mode** (Remote/Local/Agent), **Auto-apply**, **Remote state sharing**,\n  **Health** (drift detection, where available), **Locking**, **Destruction and deletion**.\n- **Registry** → publish the lesson-05 module privately (requires a VCS repo named `terraform-local-static-site`\n  with a semver tag).\n\n## Clean up\n```bash\nterraform destroy\n```\nThen delete the workspace: Settings → *Destruction and Deletion*.\n", "labFiles": [{"path": "main.tf", "content": "terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.12.0\"\n\n  # Replace YOUR-ORG with your HCP Terraform organization name, then run `terraform init`.\n  # Commented out so the repo validation script works without an HCP account.\n  #\n  # cloud {\n  #   organization = \"YOUR-ORG\"\n  #\n  #   workspaces {\n  #     name    = \"tf-associate-lab\"\n  #     project = \"Default Project\"\n  #   }\n  # }\n\n  required_providers {\n    random = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/random\"\n      version = \"~> 3.6\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"team\" {\n  description = \"Set this as a Terraform variable in the HCP Terraform workspace\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = \"platform\"\n}\n\nvariable \"api_key\" {\n  description = \"Set this as a SENSITIVE Terraform variable in the workspace\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n  default     = \"not-set\"\n}\n\nresource \"random_pet\" \"name\" {\n  prefix = var.team\n  length = 2\n}\n\n# Runs remotely: note there's no local file here. Remote runs can't write to your laptop.\nresource \"terraform_data\" \"deployment\" {\n  input = {\n    name        = random_pet.name.id\n    workspace   = terraform.workspace\n    api_key_set = var.api_key != \"not-set\"\n  }\n}\n\noutput \"deployment_name\" {\n  value = random_pet.name.id\n}\n\noutput \"api_key_configured\" {\n  value = terraform_data.deployment.output.api_key_set\n}\n"}]}], "exams": [{"id": "practice-exam-1", "title": "Practice Exam 1", "intro": "# Practice Exam 1\n\n**40 questions · 45 minutes · pass mark: 70% (aim for 85%+)**\nAnswers and explanations are at the bottom — don't scroll until you're done.\n\n---\n\n", "questions": [{"n": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes Terraform's approach to infrastructure?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Imperative — you list the exact API calls to make"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Declarative — you describe the desired end state"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Procedural — you write scripts executed in order"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Agent-based — an agent on each server pulls configuration"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Terraform is declarative.", "multi": false}, {"n": 2, "q": "Which are benefits of Infrastructure as Code? *(Select THREE)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Changes can be reviewed through version control"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Environments can be reproduced consistently"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Cloud provider bills are eliminated"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Changes can be previewed before being applied"}, {"key": "E", "text": "No need to understand the underlying platform"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "D"], "explanation": "IaC doesn't remove costs or the need to know the platform.", "multi": true}, {"n": 3, "q": "True or False: Terraform configuration written for AWS can be deployed to GCP by changing only the provider block.", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "True"}, {"key": "B", "text": "False"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Resource types are provider-specific; only the workflow is portable.", "multi": false}, {"n": 4, "q": "Which file should be committed to version control so that all team members use the same provider versions?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform.tfstate`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`.terraform/`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`.terraform.lock.hcl`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform.tfvars`"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "The dependency lock file pins provider versions and hashes.", "multi": false}, {"n": 5, "q": "A provider version constraint is `~> 4.2.0`. Which version is allowed?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "4.3.0"}, {"key": "B", "text": "4.2.9"}, {"key": "C", "text": "5.0.0"}, {"key": "D", "text": "4.1.9"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`~> 4.2.0` means `>= 4.2.0, < 4.3.0`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 6, "q": "Where does `terraform init` download providers to by default?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`~/.terraform.d/plugins`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`/usr/local/bin`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The `.terraform` directory in the working directory"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The state file"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "`.terraform/providers` in the working directory (unless a plugin cache is configured).", "multi": false}, {"n": 7, "q": "You need two AWS provider configurations for two regions. Which argument distinguishes the second one?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`name`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`alias`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`region_id`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`id`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`alias`, referenced as `aws.<alias>`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 8, "q": "Which command should you run first in a newly cloned Terraform repository?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform plan`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform validate`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform init`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform apply`"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "`init` installs providers/modules and configures the backend.", "multi": false}, {"n": 9, "q": "What does `terraform validate` check? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Syntax errors"}, {"key": "B", "text": "That referenced variables are declared"}, {"key": "C", "text": "That credentials are valid"}, {"key": "D", "text": "That the AMI ID exists"}, {"key": "E", "text": "That the state matches the real infrastructure"}], "answer": ["A", "B"], "explanation": "validate is offline: no credentials, no API lookups, no state comparison.", "multi": true}, {"n": 10, "q": "Which command would you use in CI to fail a pipeline when code is not formatted?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform fmt -diff`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform fmt -check`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform validate -fmt`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform lint`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`-check` returns non-zero if formatting is needed, without modifying files.", "multi": false}, {"n": 11, "q": "In plan output, what does `-/+` indicate?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The resource will be updated in place"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The resource will be destroyed and re-created"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The resource will be read"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The resource will be imported"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Replace: destroy then create. `+/-` is create-before-destroy.", "multi": false}, {"n": 12, "q": "You run `terraform apply tfplan` with a saved plan. What happens?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Terraform re-plans and asks for confirmation"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Terraform applies the saved plan without prompting"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Terraform errors: saved plans can only be shown"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Terraform applies only after running `validate`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Saved plans apply without a prompt.", "multi": false}, {"n": 13, "q": "Which command is equivalent to `terraform destroy`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform plan -destroy`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform apply -destroy`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform state rm -all`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform delete`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`destroy` is an alias for `apply -destroy`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 14, "q": "What is the default number of concurrent operations during apply?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "1"}, {"key": "B", "text": "5"}, {"key": "C", "text": "10"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Unlimited"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "`-parallelism` defaults to 10.", "multi": false}, {"n": 15, "q": "How do you reference the `id` attribute of a data source `aws_ami.ubuntu`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`aws_ami.ubuntu.id`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`data.ubuntu.id`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`ami.ubuntu.id`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Data sources are prefixed with `data.`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 16, "q": "A variable is set in `terraform.tfvars`, as `TF_VAR_region`, and with `-var region=...`. Which value is used?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform.tfvars`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`TF_VAR_region`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`-var`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The variable's default"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "CLI `-var`/`-var-file` has the highest precedence.", "multi": false}, {"n": 17, "q": "Which type is appropriate for a variable holding `{ name = \"web\", port = 80, public = true }`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`map(string)`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`list(any)`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`object({ name = string, port = number, public = bool })`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`set(string)`"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "Mixed types with fixed attribute names → `object`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 18, "q": "You need to create one resource per entry in `var.users = [\"alice\", \"bob\"]` and avoid replacements when an entry is removed from the middle. What do you use?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`count = length(var.users)`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`for_each = toset(var.users)`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A `dynamic` block"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`depends_on`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`for_each` gives stable addresses keyed by value.", "multi": false}, {"n": 19, "q": "Which construct generates repeated nested blocks (e.g. multiple `ingress` blocks)?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`for_each` on the resource"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`dynamic` block"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`count` on the nested block"}, {"key": "D", "text": "A `for` expression in a `locals` block"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`dynamic` blocks generate nested blocks.", "multi": false}, {"n": 20, "q": "Resource B uses `aws_instance.a.private_ip` in its arguments. What kind of dependency is this?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Explicit"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Implicit"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Circular"}, {"key": "D", "text": "None; Terraform creates them in parallel"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "References create implicit dependencies.", "multi": false}, {"n": 21, "q": "Which lifecycle argument makes Terraform return an error if a plan would destroy the resource?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`create_before_destroy`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`ignore_changes`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`prevent_destroy`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`replace_triggered_by`"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "`prevent_destroy = true`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 22, "q": "Which custom condition type only produces a warning and never blocks an apply?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`validation`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`precondition`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`postcondition`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`check` block"}], "answer": ["D"], "explanation": "`check` block assertions are warnings.", "multi": false}, {"n": 23, "q": "An output is marked `sensitive = true`. Which is TRUE?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The value is encrypted in the state file"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The value is not stored in state"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The value is redacted in CLI output but stored in plain text in state"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The value cannot be referenced by other resources"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "Sensitive only redacts output.", "multi": false}, {"n": 24, "q": "Can you define custom functions in HCL?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Yes, with a `function` block"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Yes, in `locals`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "No, only built-in functions (and provider-defined functions) are available"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Yes, using `terraform console`"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "No user-defined functions in HCL.", "multi": false}, {"n": 25, "q": "Which is a valid local module source?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`source = \"modules/vpc\"`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`source = \"./modules/vpc\"`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`source = \"local::modules/vpc\"`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`source = \"file:modules/vpc\"`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Local paths must begin with `./` or `../`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 26, "q": "A child module needs the value of the root module's `var.environment`. What must you do?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Nothing; child modules inherit variables"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Reference `parent.var.environment`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Declare a variable in the child and pass the value in the module block"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Use a `data` source"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "Modules only receive values through input variables.", "multi": false}, {"n": 27, "q": "Which argument pins a Terraform Registry module to a version?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`ref`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`version`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`tag`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`pin`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`version` (registry sources only). Git uses `?ref=`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 28, "q": "How does the root module access a value created inside a child module named `db`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`module.db.aws_db_instance.this.endpoint`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`module.db.<output_name>` via an output defined in the child"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`db.endpoint`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`local.db.endpoint`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Only outputs are exposed from a child module.", "multi": false}, {"n": 29, "q": "Where is state stored by default?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "In HCP Terraform"}, {"key": "B", "text": "In `terraform.tfstate` in the working directory"}, {"key": "C", "text": "In `.terraform/terraform.tfstate`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "In memory only"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Local backend → `terraform.tfstate` in the root module directory.", "multi": false}, {"n": 30, "q": "Which of the following can you NOT use in a `backend` block?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "String literals"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Input variables"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Boolean literals"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Partial configuration via `-backend-config`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Backend blocks can't reference variables, locals or data sources.", "multi": false}, {"n": 31, "q": "Two engineers run `terraform apply` at the same time against the same S3 backend with locking enabled. What happens?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Both applies run and the state is merged"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The second one fails to acquire the state lock"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The first one is cancelled"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The state is corrupted"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Locking prevents concurrent writes; the second operation errors (or waits with `-lock-timeout`).", "multi": false}, {"n": 32, "q": "A resource was modified manually in the console. You want to update the state to reflect reality without changing infrastructure. What do you run?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform apply -refresh-only`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform apply -replace`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform state push`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform init -reconfigure`"}], "answer": ["A"], "explanation": "Refresh-only updates state to match reality.", "multi": false}, {"n": 33, "q": "You want Terraform to stop managing a resource but leave it running. Which are valid approaches? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform destroy -target`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform state rm`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A `removed` block with `lifecycle { destroy = false }`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform taint`"}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "explanation": "Both remove from state without destroying.", "multi": true}, {"n": 34, "q": "Which block lets you rename a resource in configuration without destroying and re-creating it?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`rename`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`moved`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`import`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`alias`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`moved` blocks record address changes.", "multi": false}, {"n": 35, "q": "Which command lists all resources tracked in state?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform show -list`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform state list`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform list`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform output -all`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`terraform state list`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 36, "q": "You have an `import` block but no resource configuration yet. Which command generates the configuration?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform import -generate`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform init -import`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform show -config`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`-generate-config-out` on `plan`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 37, "q": "Which environment variable enables the most detailed logging?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`TF_LOG=DEBUG`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`TF_LOG=TRACE`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`TF_DEBUG=1`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`TF_LOG_PATH=trace`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "TRACE is the most verbose.", "multi": false}, {"n": 38, "q": "Which block connects a configuration to HCP Terraform?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`backend \"hcp\" {}`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`cloud {}` inside the `terraform` block"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`provider \"tfe\" {}`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`hcp {}`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`terraform { cloud { ... } }`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 39, "q": "When are Sentinel/OPA policies evaluated in an HCP Terraform run?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Before plan"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Between plan and apply"}, {"key": "C", "text": "After apply"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Only on destroy"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Policy checks run after plan, before apply.", "multi": false}, {"n": 40, "q": "What does an HCP Terraform workspace contain that a CLI workspace does NOT? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "State"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Variables"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Team access permissions"}, {"key": "D", "text": "A configuration directory"}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "explanation": "CLI workspaces only separate state; HCP workspaces also hold variables, permissions, run history, settings.", "multi": true}]}, {"id": "practice-exam-2", "title": "Practice Exam 2", "intro": "# Practice Exam 2\n\n**40 questions · 45 minutes · pass mark: 70% (aim for 85%+)**\nAnswers and explanations are at the bottom.\n\n---\n\n", "questions": [{"n": 1, "q": "Which Terraform component is responsible for communicating with a cloud platform's API?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Terraform Core"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The state file"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A provider plugin"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The backend"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "Providers translate resources into API calls.", "multi": false}, {"n": 2, "q": "How can Terraform help with a multi-cloud deployment?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "It automatically translates resources between clouds"}, {"key": "B", "text": "It provides one workflow and language to manage resources across providers in one configuration"}, {"key": "C", "text": "It replaces cloud-native services with generic ones"}, {"key": "D", "text": "It requires one Terraform installation per cloud"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Same workflow/language; resources are still provider-specific.", "multi": false}, {"n": 3, "q": "You omit `required_providers` and use a resource `random_pet`. What does Terraform assume?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "It fails immediately"}, {"key": "B", "text": "It installs `hashicorp/random`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "It prompts for the source"}, {"key": "D", "text": "It installs the newest community provider named random"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Terraform infers `hashicorp/<prefix>` when not declared.", "multi": false}, {"n": 4, "q": "Your lock file pins `hashicorp/aws 5.80.0`. The constraint is `~> 5.0`, and 5.95.0 is available. What does `terraform init` install?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "5.95.0"}, {"key": "B", "text": "5.80.0"}, {"key": "C", "text": "6.0.0"}, {"key": "D", "text": "It errors"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "The lock file wins until you run `init -upgrade`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 5, "q": "Which command upgrades providers to the latest versions allowed by constraints?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform upgrade`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform init -upgrade`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform providers update`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform get -update`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`init -upgrade`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 6, "q": "Which statements about state are TRUE? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "State maps resources in configuration to real-world objects"}, {"key": "B", "text": "State is optional; Terraform can work by reading the cloud APIs only"}, {"key": "C", "text": "State may contain sensitive values in plain text"}, {"key": "D", "text": "State is always encrypted by the local backend"}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "explanation": "State is required, and the local backend does not encrypt.", "multi": true}, {"n": 7, "q": "You changed a module's `version` argument. What must you run before `plan`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform validate`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform init`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform refresh`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Nothing"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Module or provider changes need `init`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 8, "q": "What does `terraform fmt` do by default?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Formats files in the current directory only"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Formats all subdirectories recursively"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Validates the configuration"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Formats state files"}], "answer": ["A"], "explanation": "Use `-recursive` for subdirectories.", "multi": false}, {"n": 9, "q": "You want the plan exit code to indicate whether there are changes. Which flag?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`-changes-only`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`-detailed-exitcode`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`-json`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`-check`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "0 = no changes, 1 = error, 2 = changes.", "multi": false}, {"n": 10, "q": "An apply fails halfway. What is true?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Terraform rolls back all changes automatically"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Successfully created resources remain and are recorded in state"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The state file is deleted"}, {"key": "D", "text": "All resources are marked tainted"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "No rollback; partial results are saved to state.", "multi": false}, {"n": 11, "q": "How do you force Terraform to replace a specific resource on the next apply?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform apply -replace=aws_instance.web`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform apply -recreate=aws_instance.web`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform state rm aws_instance.web`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform refresh aws_instance.web`"}], "answer": ["A"], "explanation": "`-replace`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 12, "q": "Which is TRUE about data sources?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "They create resources if they don't exist"}, {"key": "B", "text": "They let Terraform read information defined outside the configuration or computed by a provider"}, {"key": "C", "text": "They can only be used with the `aws` provider"}, {"key": "D", "text": "They are stored only in memory and never appear in state"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Data sources read and never manage. They are recorded in state.", "multi": false}, {"n": 13, "q": "Which variable files are loaded automatically? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform.tfvars`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`prod.tfvars`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`dev.auto.tfvars`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`variables.tf.vars`"}], "answer": ["A", "C"], "explanation": "`terraform.tfvars` and `*.auto.tfvars`; others need `-var-file`.", "multi": true}, {"n": 14, "q": "A required variable has no default and no value provided. `-input=true` (default). What happens?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Terraform uses `null`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Terraform prompts for a value"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Terraform uses an empty string"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Terraform skips resources using it"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Interactive prompt (or error with `-input=false`).", "multi": false}, {"n": 15, "q": "What does `optional(number, 1)` in an object type mean?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The attribute is required and must be 1"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The attribute may be omitted; if omitted it defaults to 1"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The attribute accepts numbers up to 1"}, {"key": "D", "text": "It's a list with one element"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Optional attribute with a default.", "multi": false}, {"n": 16, "q": "Given `var.names` is a `set(string)`, which expression is invalid?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`length(var.names)`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`var.names[0]`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`tolist(var.names)[0]`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`contains(var.names, \"a\")`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Sets aren't indexable.", "multi": false}, {"n": 17, "q": "What does `[for s in var.list : upper(s) if s != \"\"]` return?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "A map"}, {"key": "B", "text": "A list of uppercase, non-empty strings"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A set of booleans"}, {"key": "D", "text": "An error: `if` is not allowed"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "A list, with filtering by `if`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 18, "q": "What is the result of `aws_instance.web[*].id` when `count = 3`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The ID of the first instance"}, {"key": "B", "text": "A list of the three instance IDs"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A map keyed by index"}, {"key": "D", "text": "An error"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Splat returns a list.", "multi": false}, {"n": 19, "q": "Which statement about `count` and `for_each` is TRUE?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "They can be used together on the same resource"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`for_each` accepts a map or a set of strings"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`count` accepts a map"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`for_each` keys can be unknown until apply"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Map or set of strings; keys must be known at plan time; can't combine with count.", "multi": false}, {"n": 20, "q": "When should you use `depends_on`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Always, to make dependencies explicit"}, {"key": "B", "text": "When a dependency exists that Terraform cannot infer from references"}, {"key": "C", "text": "To speed up applies"}, {"key": "D", "text": "To create resources in parallel"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Hidden dependencies only.", "multi": false}, {"n": 21, "q": "Which `lifecycle` setting avoids downtime by creating a replacement before destroying the original?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`prevent_destroy`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`create_before_destroy`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`ignore_changes`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`replace_triggered_by`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`create_before_destroy`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 22, "q": "Where can you use `self` in a custom condition?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "In a variable `validation`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "In a `precondition`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "In a `postcondition`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "In a `check` block assertion"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "`self` is available in postconditions (and provisioners).", "multi": false}, {"n": 23, "q": "Which options keep a secret out of the state file? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`sensitive = true` on a variable"}, {"key": "B", "text": "An ephemeral variable/resource"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A write-only argument"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Wrapping the value in `nonsensitive()`"}], "answer": ["B", "C"], "explanation": "`sensitive` only redacts; `nonsensitive` removes redaction.", "multi": true}, {"n": 24, "q": "How does HashiCorp Vault improve secrets handling with Terraform?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "It encrypts `.tf` files"}, {"key": "B", "text": "It can issue short-lived dynamic credentials that Terraform reads at run time"}, {"key": "C", "text": "It replaces the state backend"}, {"key": "D", "text": "It removes the need for providers"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Dynamic, short-lived secrets reduce exposure.", "multi": false}, {"n": 25, "q": "Which is a valid Git module source pinned to a tag?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`source = \"git::https://example.com/net.git\" version = \"v1.0.0\"`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`source = \"git::https://example.com/net.git?ref=v1.0.0\"`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`source = \"git::https://example.com/net.git#v1.0.0\"`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`source = \"git::https://example.com/net.git@v1.0.0\"`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`?ref=` for Git; `version` is registry-only.", "multi": false}, {"n": 26, "q": "What is the naming convention for a module repository published to the public registry?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`module-<name>-<provider>`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform-<provider>-<name>`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`<provider>-terraform-<name>`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Any name"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`terraform-<PROVIDER>-<NAME>`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 27, "q": "Child modules and providers — which is TRUE?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Child modules must always declare their own `provider` blocks"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Child modules inherit default provider configurations from the parent; aliased ones must be passed with `providers`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Providers can't be used in modules"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Each module downloads its own provider binary"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Default configs are inherited; aliases are passed explicitly.", "multi": false}, {"n": 28, "q": "Which command downloads modules but does not initialize backends or providers?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform get`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform fetch`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform modules`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform init -modules-only`"}], "answer": ["A"], "explanation": "`terraform get`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 29, "q": "Which is a benefit of remote state backends? *(Select TWO)*", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Team members share the same state"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Locking prevents concurrent writes (backend-dependent)"}, {"key": "C", "text": "They make state files human-editable"}, {"key": "D", "text": "They remove secrets from state"}], "answer": ["A", "B"], "explanation": "Remote state is still sensitive and must not be hand-edited.", "multi": true}, {"n": 30, "q": "You changed the backend from local to S3. What command migrates the existing state?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform state push`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform init -migrate-state`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform apply -migrate`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform backend migrate`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`init -migrate-state` (init prompts to migrate).", "multi": false}, {"n": 31, "q": "A crashed CI job left a stale lock on state. How do you remove it?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform unlock`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Delete `.terraform/`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform state rm lock`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`force-unlock` with the lock ID.", "multi": false}, {"n": 32, "q": "Which data source reads outputs from another configuration's state?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform_state`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform_remote_state`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`remote_outputs`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`state_outputs`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`terraform_remote_state` (root outputs only).", "multi": false}, {"n": 33, "q": "What is the preferred replacement for `terraform taint`?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform untaint`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform apply -replace=ADDR`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform state rm`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform refresh`"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`-replace` shows the replacement in the plan.", "multi": false}, {"n": 34, "q": "You ran `terraform import aws_s3_bucket.logs my-logs`. What happened?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The bucket configuration was written to `main.tf`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The bucket was added to state; config must already exist"}, {"key": "C", "text": "A new bucket was created"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The bucket was destroyed and recreated"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "CLI import only writes state.", "multi": false}, {"n": 35, "q": "Which command shows every attribute of a single resource in state?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "`terraform state show ADDR`"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`terraform show ADDR`"}, {"key": "C", "text": "`terraform output ADDR`"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`terraform state pull ADDR`"}], "answer": ["A"], "explanation": "`state show`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 36, "q": "You set `TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log` but no log file is created. Why?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The path must be absolute"}, {"key": "B", "text": "`TF_LOG` is not set"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Logs are only written on crash"}, {"key": "D", "text": "`TF_LOG_PATH` only works in HCP Terraform"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "`TF_LOG_PATH` requires `TF_LOG`.", "multi": false}, {"n": 37, "q": "What happens to a pull request opened against a VCS-connected HCP Terraform workspace?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "It's applied automatically"}, {"key": "B", "text": "A speculative plan runs and its result is reported to the PR"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Nothing until merged"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The workspace is locked"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Speculative plan (plan-only).", "multi": false}, {"n": 38, "q": "Which Sentinel enforcement level can be overridden by an authorized user?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "advisory"}, {"key": "B", "text": "soft-mandatory"}, {"key": "C", "text": "hard-mandatory"}, {"key": "D", "text": "none"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Soft-mandatory can be overridden; hard-mandatory cannot; advisory only warns.", "multi": false}, {"n": 39, "q": "What organizes HCP Terraform workspaces into groups for access management?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Tags"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Projects"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Teams"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Variable sets"}], "answer": ["B"], "explanation": "Projects.", "multi": false}, {"n": 40, "q": "Which HCP Terraform execution mode lets runs reach resources in a private data center?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "Remote"}, {"key": "B", "text": "Local"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Agent"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Speculative"}], "answer": ["C"], "explanation": "Agents run inside your network.", "multi": false}]}], "flashcards": [{"n": 1, "q": "Terraform style: declarative or imperative?", "a": "Declarative"}, {"n": 2, "q": "Language Terraform config is written in", "a": "HCL (or JSON `*.tf.json`)"}, {"n": 3, "q": "Self-hosted HCP Terraform", "a": "Terraform Enterprise"}, {"n": 4, "q": "Component that calls platform APIs", "a": "Provider"}, {"n": 5, "q": "Provider source default hostname", "a": "`registry.terraform.io`"}, {"n": 6, "q": "Provider source format", "a": "`[hostname/]namespace/type`"}, {"n": 7, "q": "`~> 1.2.0` allows", "a": "`>= 1.2.0, < 1.3.0`"}, {"n": 8, "q": "`~> 1.2` allows", "a": "`>= 1.2, < 2.0`"}, {"n": 9, "q": "File pinning provider versions + hashes", "a": "`.terraform.lock.hcl` (commit it)"}, {"n": 10, "q": "Does lock file track modules?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 11, "q": "Upgrade providers within constraints", "a": "`terraform init -upgrade`"}, {"n": 12, "q": "Multiple configs of same provider", "a": "`alias` + `provider = aws.alias`"}, {"n": 13, "q": "Pass aliased provider to module", "a": "`providers = { aws = aws.west }`"}, {"n": 14, "q": "Provider tiers", "a": "Official, Partner, Community (+ Archived)"}, {"n": 15, "q": "Where `init` puts providers", "a": "`.terraform/providers`"}, {"n": 16, "q": "First command in a new dir", "a": "`terraform init`"}, {"n": 17, "q": "`validate` contacts APIs?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 18, "q": "`fmt` flag for CI", "a": "`-check`"}, {"n": 19, "q": "Format subdirectories", "a": "`fmt -recursive`"}, {"n": 20, "q": "`-/+` in plan", "a": "Destroy then create (replace)"}, {"n": 21, "q": "`+/-` in plan", "a": "Create then destroy (create_before_destroy)"}, {"n": 22, "q": "`<=` in plan", "a": "Read data source"}, {"n": 23, "q": "Default parallelism", "a": "10"}, {"n": 24, "q": "Apply saved plan prompts?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 25, "q": "`destroy` equivalent", "a": "`apply -destroy`"}, {"n": 26, "q": "Preview destroy", "a": "`plan -destroy`"}, {"n": 27, "q": "Plan exit codes with `-detailed-exitcode`", "a": "0 none, 1 error, 2 changes"}, {"n": 28, "q": "Rollback on failed apply?", "a": "No; partial state saved"}, {"n": 29, "q": "Data source reference", "a": "`data.TYPE.NAME.ATTR`"}, {"n": 30, "q": "Module output reference", "a": "`module.NAME.OUTPUT`"}, {"n": 31, "q": "Highest variable precedence", "a": "`-var` / `-var-file` on CLI"}, {"n": 32, "q": "Lowest variable precedence", "a": "`default`"}, {"n": 33, "q": "Env var for variable `region`", "a": "`TF_VAR_region`"}, {"n": 34, "q": "Auto-loaded var files", "a": "`terraform.tfvars(.json)`, `*.auto.tfvars(.json)`"}, {"n": 35, "q": "Missing required var & interactive", "a": "Prompt"}, {"n": 36, "q": "Unordered unique collection", "a": "`set`"}, {"n": 37, "q": "Fixed-attribute mixed-type structure", "a": "`object`"}, {"n": 38, "q": "Fixed-length positional mixed types", "a": "`tuple`"}, {"n": 39, "q": "Repeat nested blocks", "a": "`dynamic` block"}, {"n": 40, "q": "`for_each` accepts", "a": "map or set of strings"}, {"n": 41, "q": "Iterate & keep stable addresses", "a": "`for_each`"}, {"n": 42, "q": "Conditional resource", "a": "`count = cond ? 1 : 0`"}, {"n": 43, "q": "`count` + `for_each` together?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 44, "q": "Hidden dependency", "a": "`depends_on`"}, {"n": 45, "q": "Zero-downtime replacement", "a": "`create_before_destroy`"}, {"n": 46, "q": "Block destroy in plan", "a": "`prevent_destroy`"}, {"n": 47, "q": "Ignore drift on attribute", "a": "`ignore_changes`"}, {"n": 48, "q": "Replace when another thing changes", "a": "`replace_triggered_by`"}, {"n": 49, "q": "Custom condition that only warns", "a": "`check` block"}, {"n": 50, "q": "Condition with `self`", "a": "`postcondition`"}, {"n": 51, "q": "`sensitive = true` keeps value out of state?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 52, "q": "Keep value out of state & plan", "a": "`ephemeral` / write-only args"}, {"n": 53, "q": "Short-lived cloud creds from Vault", "a": "Dynamic secrets"}, {"n": 54, "q": "User-defined functions in HCL?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 55, "q": "Replacement for `null_resource`", "a": "`terraform_data`"}, {"n": 56, "q": "Provisioner failure default", "a": "Fail → resource tainted"}, {"n": 57, "q": "Module definition", "a": "Directory of `.tf` files"}, {"n": 58, "q": "Local module path must start with", "a": "`./` or `../`"}, {"n": 59, "q": "`version` arg works for", "a": "Registry modules only"}, {"n": 60, "q": "Pin Git module", "a": "`?ref=v1.2.0`"}, {"n": 61, "q": "Subdirectory in module source", "a": "`//subdir`"}, {"n": 62, "q": "Registry module repo name", "a": "`terraform-<PROVIDER>-<NAME>`"}, {"n": 63, "q": "Child sees parent vars?", "a": "No — pass as inputs"}, {"n": 64, "q": "Download modules only", "a": "`terraform get`"}, {"n": 65, "q": "Default backend", "a": "local"}, {"n": 66, "q": "Default state file", "a": "`terraform.tfstate`"}, {"n": 67, "q": "Previous state copy", "a": "`terraform.tfstate.backup`"}, {"n": 68, "q": "Variables in backend block?", "a": "No; use `-backend-config`"}, {"n": 69, "q": "Move state to new backend", "a": "`init -migrate-state`"}, {"n": 70, "q": "Release stuck lock", "a": "`force-unlock LOCK_ID`"}, {"n": 71, "q": "Read other state outputs", "a": "`terraform_remote_state` (root outputs only)"}, {"n": 72, "q": "HCP alternative", "a": "`tfe_outputs`"}, {"n": 73, "q": "Accept real-world changes into state", "a": "`apply -refresh-only`"}, {"n": 74, "q": "Replacement for `taint`", "a": "`apply -replace=ADDR`"}, {"n": 75, "q": "Rename without recreate", "a": "`moved` block"}, {"n": 76, "q": "Forget resource, keep object", "a": "`removed` block / `state rm`"}, {"n": 77, "q": "State field incremented each write", "a": "`serial`"}, {"n": 78, "q": "Unique ID of state history", "a": "`lineage`"}, {"n": 79, "q": "Is state encrypted locally?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 80, "q": "CLI workspace state dir", "a": "`terraform.tfstate.d/`"}, {"n": 81, "q": "Current workspace in HCL", "a": "`terraform.workspace`"}, {"n": 82, "q": "Config-driven import", "a": "`import { to  id }`"}, {"n": 83, "q": "Generate import config", "a": "`plan -generate-config-out=f.tf`"}, {"n": 84, "q": "CLI import writes config?", "a": "No"}, {"n": 85, "q": "Import changes", "a": "State only"}, {"n": 86, "q": "Most verbose log level", "a": "TRACE"}, {"n": 87, "q": "Log to file", "a": "`TF_LOG_PATH` (+ `TF_LOG`)"}, {"n": 88, "q": "Provider-only logs", "a": "`TF_LOG_PROVIDER`"}, {"n": 89, "q": "Panic output file", "a": "`crash.log`"}, {"n": 90, "q": "List state resources", "a": "`terraform state list`"}, {"n": 91, "q": "One resource's attributes", "a": "`terraform state show ADDR`"}, {"n": 92, "q": "Store HCP API token", "a": "`terraform login`"}, {"n": 93, "q": "HCP integration block", "a": "`cloud {}` in `terraform {}`"}, {"n": 94, "q": "HCP ws vs CLI ws", "a": "HCP: state + vars + perms + runs; CLI: state only"}, {"n": 95, "q": "Group HCP workspaces", "a": "Projects"}, {"n": 96, "q": "Share vars across workspaces", "a": "Variable sets"}, {"n": 97, "q": "PR in VCS workflow triggers", "a": "Speculative plan"}, {"n": 98, "q": "Policy frameworks", "a": "Sentinel, OPA"}, {"n": 99, "q": "When policies run", "a": "After plan, before apply"}, {"n": 100, "q": "Non-overridable policy level", "a": "hard-mandatory"}, {"n": 101, "q": "Overridable policy level", "a": "soft-mandatory"}, {"n": 102, "q": "Reach private networks", "a": "HCP Terraform agents"}, {"n": 103, "q": "Execution modes", "a": "Remote, Local, Agent"}, {"n": 104, "q": "Periodic drift detection", "a": "Health assessments"}, {"n": 105, "q": "Share private modules", "a": "Private registry"}, {"n": 106, "q": "Secretless cloud auth in HCP", "a": "Dynamic provider credentials (OIDC)"}], "cheatsheets": [{"id": "cli", "title": "Terraform CLI Cheatsheet", "md": "# Terraform CLI Cheatsheet\n\n## Workflow\n```bash\nterraform init                       # backend + providers + modules; re-run after changes to any of them\nterraform init -upgrade              # newest allowed provider/module versions\nterraform init -migrate-state        # move state to a new backend\nterraform init -reconfigure          # re-init backend, ignore existing config\nterraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl\n\nterraform fmt [-recursive] [-check] [-diff]\nterraform validate [-json]\n\nterraform plan [-out=tfplan] [-var k=v] [-var-file=f.tfvars]\nterraform plan -destroy | -refresh-only | -refresh=false\nterraform plan -replace=ADDR | -target=ADDR\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode    # 0 none, 1 error, 2 changes\nterraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf\n\nterraform apply [tfplan] [-auto-approve] [-parallelism=10]\nterraform apply -refresh-only\nterraform apply -replace=ADDR\nterraform destroy [-auto-approve]    # == terraform apply -destroy\n```\n\n## Inspect\n```bash\nterraform show [tfplan] [-json]\nterraform output [NAME] [-raw|-json]\nterraform state list [FILTER]\nterraform state show ADDR\nterraform state pull\nterraform console\nterraform graph | dot -Tsvg > graph.svg\nterraform providers\nterraform version\n```\n\n## Modify state (prefer config blocks where possible)\n```bash\nterraform state mv SRC DST                  # prefer: moved {}\nterraform state rm ADDR                     # prefer: removed {}\nterraform import ADDR ID                    # prefer: import {}\nterraform state push FILE                   # dangerous\nterraform state replace-provider OLD NEW\nterraform force-unlock LOCK_ID\nterraform taint ADDR / untaint ADDR         # deprecated → -replace\nterraform refresh                           # deprecated → apply -refresh-only\n```\n\n## Workspaces (CLI)\n```bash\nterraform workspace list | show | new NAME | select NAME | delete NAME\n```\n\n## HCP Terraform\n```bash\nterraform login [hostname]\nterraform logout\n```\n\n## Providers\n```bash\nterraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64\nterraform providers mirror ./mirror\n```\n\n## Testing\n```bash\nterraform test                       # runs *.tftest.hcl\n```\n\n## Environment variables\n| Var | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `TF_LOG` | TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, OFF, JSON |\n| `TF_LOG_CORE` / `TF_LOG_PROVIDER` | Per-component log level |\n| `TF_LOG_PATH` | Log file (needs `TF_LOG`) |\n| `TF_VAR_name` | Set input variable `name` |\n| `TF_INPUT=0` | Disable interactive prompts |\n| `TF_CLI_ARGS`, `TF_CLI_ARGS_plan` | Extra CLI args |\n| `TF_DATA_DIR` | Change `.terraform` location |\n| `TF_WORKSPACE` | Select workspace |\n| `TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` | Shared provider cache |\n| `TF_CLOUD_ORGANIZATION`, `TF_CLOUD_HOSTNAME` | `cloud` block defaults |\n| `TF_IN_AUTOMATION` | Less interactive-oriented output |\n\n## Files\n| File | Commit to Git? |\n|---|---|\n| `*.tf` | ✅ |\n| `.terraform.lock.hcl` | ✅ |\n| `.terraform/` | ❌ |\n| `terraform.tfstate`, `*.tfstate.backup` | ❌ |\n| `*.tfvars` with secrets | ❌ |\n| `crash.log` | ❌ |\n| Saved plan files | ❌ (may contain secrets) |\n"}, {"id": "hcl", "title": "HCL Language Cheatsheet", "md": "# HCL Language Cheatsheet\n\n## Block types\n```hcl\nterraform { required_version = \"...\"  required_providers {...}  backend \"x\" {...} | cloud {...} }\nprovider \"aws\" { region = \"...\"  alias = \"west\" }\nresource \"TYPE\" \"NAME\" { ... }          # TYPE.NAME\ndata \"TYPE\" \"NAME\" { ... }              # data.TYPE.NAME\nvariable \"NAME\" { type default description sensitive nullable ephemeral validation {} }\noutput \"NAME\" { value description sensitive ephemeral depends_on precondition {} }\nlocals { k = v }                        # local.k\nmodule \"NAME\" { source version providers count for_each depends_on }\nmoved { from = ...  to = ... }\nremoved { from = ...  lifecycle { destroy = false } }\nimport { to = ...  id = \"...\" }\ncheck \"NAME\" { data \"...\" \"...\" {}  assert { condition  error_message } }\nephemeral \"TYPE\" \"NAME\" { ... }         # ephemeral.TYPE.NAME, never persisted\n```\n\n## Types\n`string` `number` `bool` · `list(T)` `set(T)` `map(T)` · `object({...})` `tuple([...])` · `any` · `null`\n`optional(T, default)` inside `object`.\n\n## Variable precedence (last wins)\ndefault → `TF_VAR_x` → `terraform.tfvars` → `terraform.tfvars.json` → `*.auto.tfvars(.json)` → `-var` / `-var-file`\n\n## Expressions\n```hcl\n\"${var.a}-${local.b}\"                                    # interpolation\n\"%{ if cond }yes%{ else }no%{ endif }\"                   # directive\ncond ? a : b                                             # conditional\n[for x in list : f(x) if cond]                           # list comprehension\n{for k, v in map : k => v}                               # map comprehension\n{for x in list : x.group => x...}                        # grouping (ellipsis)\nresource.name[*].attr                                    # splat\n<<-EOT ... EOT                                            # heredoc (indented)\n```\n\n## Meta-arguments\n| Arg | On | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `count` | resource, module, data | `count.index`; `[*]` splat |\n| `for_each` | resource, module, data | map or set(string); `each.key`, `each.value` |\n| `depends_on` | resource, module, data, output | hidden deps only |\n| `provider` | resource, data | `aws.west` |\n| `providers` | module | `{ aws = aws.west }` |\n| `lifecycle` | resource | `create_before_destroy`, `prevent_destroy`, `ignore_changes`, `replace_triggered_by`, `precondition`, `postcondition` |\n\n## dynamic block\n```hcl\ndynamic \"ingress\" {\n  for_each = var.ports\n  iterator = port          # optional; default = block name\n  content {\n    from_port = port.value\n    to_port   = port.value\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Custom conditions\n| | Blocks run? | `self`? |\n|---|---|---|\n| variable `validation` | yes | no (use `var.x`) |\n| `precondition` | yes | no |\n| `postcondition` | yes | **yes** |\n| `check` `assert` | **no, warning** | no |\n\n## Handy functions\n```\nlength  merge  concat  flatten  keys  values  lookup(m,k,default)  contains  distinct\nelement(list, i)  slice  zipmap  toset  tolist  tomap  coalesce  one\njoin(sep, list)  split(sep, s)  upper  lower  format  replace  trimspace  regex  startswith  strcontains\nfile  templatefile(path, vars)  fileexists  jsonencode  jsondecode  yamlencode  base64encode\ncidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 1) → \"10.0.1.0/24\"   cidrhost\nmin  max  ceil  floor  abs\ntry(expr, fallback)  can(expr)\ntimestamp  formatdate  uuid  sha256  md5\n```\n\n## Named values\n`var.` `local.` `module.X.` `data.` `path.module` `path.root` `path.cwd` `terraform.workspace` `count.index` `each.key` `each.value` `self`\n\n## Provisioners (last resort)\n```hcl\nprovisioner \"local-exec\"  { command = \"echo ${self.id}\" }\nprovisioner \"remote-exec\" { inline = [\"sudo apt update\"] }   # needs connection {}\nprovisioner \"file\"        { source = \"a\" destination = \"/tmp/a\" }\n# when = destroy     on_failure = continue | fail (default → tainted)\n```\n"}, {"id": "state-and-backends", "title": "State & Backends Cheatsheet", "md": "# State & Backends Cheatsheet\n\n## Defaults\n- Backend: **local** → `./terraform.tfstate` (+ `terraform.tfstate.backup`)\n- CLI workspace states: `terraform.tfstate.d/<name>/terraform.tfstate`\n- Refresh happens on every `plan`/`apply` unless `-refresh=false`\n\n## Purpose of state\n1. Map config ↔ real objects  2. Metadata (dependencies)  3. Performance cache  4. Team sync (remote)\n\n## Backend rules\n- One backend per configuration; `cloud` and `backend` are mutually exclusive\n- No variables/locals/data in backend blocks → partial config via `-backend-config`\n- Change backend → `terraform init` (`-migrate-state` / `-reconfigure`)\n- Locking automatic where supported; `-lock=false`, `-lock-timeout=30s`, `force-unlock ID`\n\n## Common backends\n| Backend | Locking |\n|---|---|\n| local | ✅ (system file locks) |\n| s3 | ✅ `use_lockfile = true` (S3 native), DynamoDB (deprecated) |\n| azurerm | ✅ blob lease |\n| gcs | ✅ |\n| consul | ✅ |\n| pg, kubernetes | ✅ |\n| http | optional |\n| HCP Terraform (`cloud`) | ✅ built-in |\n\n## Drift decision tree\n```\nReal infra changed outside Terraform\n├── Keep the manual change?      → update config to match, then plan shows no changes\n│                                  (or apply -refresh-only to accept into state first)\n├── Revert to config?            → terraform apply\n├── Attribute managed elsewhere? → lifecycle { ignore_changes = [attr] }\n└── Object deleted?              → plan will recreate it\n```\n\n## Refactoring matrix\n| Goal | Config (preferred) | CLI |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rename / move address | `moved {}` | `terraform state mv` |\n| Stop managing (keep object) | `removed { lifecycle { destroy = false } }` | `terraform state rm` |\n| Adopt existing object | `import {}` + `plan -generate-config-out` | `terraform import` |\n| Force re-create | — | `apply -replace=ADDR` (was `taint`) |\n| Sync state with reality | — | `apply -refresh-only` (was `refresh`) |\n"}]};
